// Auto-generated data file for code-review-reference.html Bug Corpus tab.
// Each entry is the raw <tr>...</tr> HTML for one corpus row.
// To add a new row: append one JSON-escaped string to this array.
const MISS_ROWS = ["<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">divergent frame readers (stall path skips dispatch)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>The h2 server reads frames in TWO places — <code>serve</code>&rarr;<code>dispatch_frame</code> (main loop) and <code>send_body</code>&rarr;<code>pump_for_window</code> (flow-control stall). <code>pump_for_window</code> reimplemented a SUBSET of dispatch (control frames + RST) and dropped everything else, so a frame arriving while a response was stalled — e.g. HEADERS for an over-limit stream sent before the unblocking WINDOW_UPDATE — was never HPACK-decoded (decoder desync) and the over-limit stream was never <code>RST(REFUSED_STREAM)</code>'d. PR2's refuse/idle/closed rules applied only on the main path. Codex P2 on #27589. Fix: <code>pump_for_window</code> delegates to <code>dispatch_frame</code>; enforce half-closed-remote (§5.1 — DATA/HEADERS after END_STREAM &rarr; STREAM_CLOSED) which also blocks <code>run_request</code> re-entrancy; <code>send_body</code> stops gracefully if its stream is reset mid-stall. Incidentally closed h2spec 5.1.2. <strong>Lesson: when adding a rule for an event, grep EVERY reader of that event (<code>read_frame</code> callers), not just the path in front of you; a second reader that hand-picks a subset is the smell — unify the readers.</strong> Commit <code>88a5d3435a</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_pump_delegates_dispatch.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">server stream-state: even id misclassified closed</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2ServerConn.classify_stream</code> decided idle-vs-closed by <code>id &le; last_stream_id</code> alone, but <code>last_stream_id</code> only advances for client-initiated (<strong>odd</strong>) stream ids. A never-opened <strong>even</strong> (server-initiated) id &mdash; e.g. DATA/RST_STREAM/WINDOW_UPDATE on stream 2 after the client opened odd stream 3 &mdash; was classified <code>.closed</code>, so the server answered DATA with <code>RST_STREAM(STREAM_CLOSED)</code> and silently ignored RST/WINDOW_UPDATE, instead of the connection-level <code>PROTOCOL_ERROR</code> RFC 9113 §5.1 requires for a frame on an idle stream. This server never opens push streams, so an even id is ALWAYS idle. Codex P2 on #27589 (discussion_r3488271404). Fix: gate <code>.closed</code> on <code>stream_id &amp; 1 == 1 &amp;&amp; stream_id &le; last_stream_id</code>; every other unopened id is idle. Repro: detector flags <code>classify_stream</code> on <code>e97d398e02</code>, clean on <code>6024da169c</code>. <strong>Lesson:</strong> a stream-state classifier keyed on a monotonic counter must also test id PARITY &mdash; the counter advances over only one peer's id space, so &ldquo;below the counter&rdquo; still contains never-opened ids of the opposite parity. Commit <code>6024da169c</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_stream_state_even_idle.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">server trailers conn-vs-stream scope</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2ServerConn.on_trailers</code> rejected a trailer section lacking END_STREAM with a bare <code>return error</code>, which bubbles through <code>serve()</code>&rarr;<code>send_goaway</code> and tears down the WHOLE connection, when every other malformed-request path in the same PR uses <code>send_rst_stream(.protocol_error)</code> (stream error, RFC 9113 §8.1.1). One bad request stream killed all streams. Codex P2 on #27569. <strong>Why missed:</strong> <code>detect_error_scope.sh</code> targets exactly this class, but its <code>READER_FNS</code> list predated the server trailer/continuation/finalize handlers (<code>on_trailers</code>/<code>finalize_trailers</code>/<code>finalize_headers</code>/<code>on_continuation</code> weren't listed), so it never flagged line 437 &mdash; AND the detector wasn't even run (abbreviated review). Fix (code): RST the stream; decode the trailer block BEFORE the well-formedness check so a reject doesn't desync HPACK for later streams. Fix (process): added the server handlers to <code>READER_FNS</code>. Repro: detector flags <code>on_trailers</code> on <code>cc2d9b873c</code>, clean on <code>51c8732de8</code>. Commit <code>51c8732de8</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_error_scope.sh</code> (READER_FNS extended)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">HPACK decode failure RST'd (stream) instead of GOAWAY (connection)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2ServerConn.finalize_headers</code>/<code>finalize_trailers</code> answered a <code>c.decoder.decode(...)</code> failure with <code>send_rst_stream(s.id, .compression_error)</code> (per-stream). But <code>H2HpackDecoder.decode</code> mutates the dynamic table AS it processes the block, so a failure leaves the decoder out of sync with the peer &mdash; every later header block on the connection decodes to garbage. RFC 9113 §4.3: a decode error MUST be a CONNECTION error of type COMPRESSION_ERROR (GOAWAY + close); the RST kept a corrupted connection alive. Codex P2 on #27569. <strong>This is the INVERSE of <code>detect_error_scope.sh</code></strong> (which flags conn-errors that should be per-stream) &mdash; that detector can't catch a per-stream handler for a fault that must be connection-level, so a new detector was needed. (The <code>finalize_headers</code> RST predated the PR &mdash; the h2spec baseline already recorded it as the PR3 hpack cluster &mdash; but the PR replicated it in the new <code>finalize_trailers</code>.) Fix: <code>send_goaway(.compression_error)</code> + <code>return</code> at both sites. <strong>Bonus:</strong> closed the whole h2spec HPACK error-scope cluster &mdash; 9 cases, 120&rarr;129 pass. <strong>Lesson:</strong> error-scope review is bidirectional &mdash; ask BOTH &ldquo;is this conn-error really per-stream?&rdquo; AND &ldquo;is this per-stream RST really a connection error?&rdquo; (any fault that desyncs connection-global state, like the HPACK table). Commit <code>d6cd7f8118</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_hpack_decode_scope.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">server content-length validated only on last occurrence</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2ServerConn.build_request</code> looped over header fields doing <code>content_length = f.value.int()</code> per <code>content-length</code> field &mdash; the last occurrence overwrote earlier ones, so <code>content-length: 5</code> then <code>content-length: 0</code> with an empty body was accepted, and both conflicting fields were still added to <code>req.header</code>. RFC 9110 §8.6: differing duplicate content-lengths are malformed. Codex P2 on #27569. <strong>Why missed:</strong> Angle A on a loop that assigns a singleton-field value must ask &ldquo;what if it appears twice with different values?&rdquo; &mdash; the abbreviated review didn't (angles not actually run). Class: a repeatable-on-the-wire field that must be singleton, extracted with <code>x = f.value</code> in a loop without a conflict check. Fix: reject a second content-length whose value differs from the first. Commit <code>51c8732de8</code>.</td>\n      <td>Angle A guard-completeness (singleton-in-loop)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">HEADERS after END_STREAM appended as trailers</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>on_response_headers</code> treated any second-or-later HEADERS block as trailers, gating only on <code>was_headers_done</code>. A HEADERS frame after the stream already ended (a prior END_STREAM set <code>s.ended</code>) is a frame on a <strong>closed stream</strong> (RFC 9113 §5.1), not trailers &mdash; with back-to-back trailer + extra HEADERS in one read buffer, the reader appends the extra fields to <code>resp_trailers</code> before <code>wait_response</code> flushes, delivering data the peer sent after closing the stream. <strong>The sibling DATA path already guarded on <code>s.ended</code>; the HEADERS path didn't</strong> &mdash; a sibling-handler gap. Codex P2 on #27413. Fix: early <code>if s.ended { reset_stream(.stream_closed); return }</code> before the trailers handling (legitimate trailers unaffected &mdash; <code>s.ended</code> is set afterwards). Reproduced statically; no deterministic runtime repro (<code>finish_stream</code> races the reader). <strong>Lesson:</strong> when one handler arm gains a state-guard, grep the sibling arms for the same guard. Commit <code>dd76a2eb3b</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_mux_headers_after_end.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">sync/mux parity: response trailers dropped</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>A muxed response followed by a trailing HEADERS block (trailers &mdash; <code>grpc-status</code>, <code>digest</code>, ...) was HPACK-decoded by <code>on_response_headers</code> (it must, to keep decoder state in sync) but the second-HEADERS branch only set <code>s.ended</code> (&ldquo;only its END_STREAM matters for this client&rdquo;) and <strong>dropped every decoded field</strong>, so <code>H2MuxConn</code> callers lost trailer metadata while the stream completed &ldquo;normally&rdquo;. The sync <code>H2Conn.read_response</code> appends non-pseudo trailer fields to <code>H2ClientResponse.headers</code> &mdash; <strong>same response-parity class as the 1xx+END_STREAM gap, but the OPPOSITE direction</strong>: there the sync path was missing a check the mux path had; here the mux path was missing behaviour the sync path had. Codex P2 on #27413 (discussion_r3477245531). Fix: per-stream <code>resp_trailers</code> list &mdash; the trailers branch (<code>was_headers_done</code>) appends the non-pseudo fields, and <code>wait_response</code> flushes them into <code>resp.headers</code> under <code>s.mu</code> when the stream ends, mirroring the sync path (pseudo-header fields are forbidden in trailers and dropped). <strong>Subtlety that shaped the fix:</strong> trailers can NOT just be appended to <code>s.resp_headers</code> &mdash; <code>wait_response</code> snapshots that list ONCE when <code>headers_done</code> first becomes true, so a later append is silently missed; a separate list flushed at stream-end is required. <strong>Lesson: sync/mux parity cuts BOTH ways</strong> &mdash; when two parallel implementations exist, audit what EACH path does that the other doesn't, not just newly-added checks. Commit <code>dde05d7c80</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_mux_trailers_preserved.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">H2MuxConn reader leak: optional <code>close_transport</code></span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>new_h2_mux_conn(transport, close_transport)</code> documented <code>close_transport</code> as <em>optional</em> (default <code>unsafe { nil }</code>). The reader thread spawned in the constructor blocks in <code>transport.read()</code>; the <code>H2Transport</code> interface is read/write <strong>only</strong> (no <code>close()</code> &mdash; by design, so <code>SSLConn</code> satisfies it directly via <code>shutdown()</code>), so the <code>close_transport</code> callback is the SOLE way to interrupt that blocking read at teardown. <code>teardown_transport</code> guards the call with <code>close_transport != unsafe { nil }</code>, so a <strong>nil</strong> closer makes teardown a silent no-op: an idle retirement (<code>shutdown_when_idle</code>) or last-reference <code>release()</code> leaks the reader thread AND the connection forever (the reader only sets <code>closed</code> after a read error/timeout, which never arrives on a silent transport). <code>drop_ref</code>'s own comment warns of exactly this leak &mdash; but only the non-nil path avoids it; the 28 hermetic tests masked it by closing the pipe manually. Codex P2 on #27413. Fix: <strong>require a non-nil closer</strong> &mdash; <code>new_h2_mux_conn</code> panics on nil before spawning the reader, so the teardown&rarr;closer&rarr;reader-exit chain always exists (can't add <code>close()</code> to the interface without breaking &ldquo;SSLConn satisfies H2Transport directly&rdquo;). <strong>Lesson:</strong> a spawned thread blocked on a blocking read can only be woken by closing its transport; if that close mechanism is optional/absent, teardown silently leaks the thread &mdash; make the closer required, not optional. <strong>Phase R note:</strong> reproduced STATICALLY (after the fix a nil closer panics, so it can't be exercised in a V test &mdash; panic aborts the binary); the runtime <code>test_mux_release_wakes_reader_via_closer</code> is a complementary teardown-chain guard, NOT the nil repro (it uses a real closer that worked on the buggy commit too). Commit <code>6e5a024922</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_mux_requires_closer.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>peer_max_frame</code> SETTER not serialized with writes</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><strong>The completion of the <code>peer_max_frame</code> TOCTOU family</strong> (the prior fixes hardened the reader/sender side; this is the setter side). The senders re-read <code>peer_max_frame</code> under <code>fmu</code>, <strong>release <code>fmu</code></strong>, then write the frame under <code>wmu</code> (held across both) &mdash; so the re-read only <em>minimises</em> the window (comments admit \"to minimise the TOCTOU window\"). The residual race stayed open because the SETTER, <code>apply_peer_settings</code>'s <code>h2_settings_max_frame_size</code> arm, updated <code>peer_max_frame</code> under <code>fmu</code>-<strong>only</strong>: a lowered <code>SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE</code> could land in the sender's <code>fmu</code>-release &rarr; write gap, emitting an oversized frame &rarr; peer <code>FRAME_SIZE_ERROR</code>. Codex P2 on #27413. Fix: update <code>peer_max_frame</code> under <code>wmu</code> (then <code>fmu</code>) so it serializes with the senders, mirroring the <code>header_table_size</code> arm (already <code>wmu</code>-fenced). <code>apply_peer_settings</code> holds no lock at entry, so <code>wmu&rarr;fmu</code> can't invert. <strong>Lesson:</strong> a reader-side recheck only closes a TOCTOU if the WRITER of that state holds the lock the action is fenced by &mdash; trace the SETTER, not just the reader. Commit <code>f873315d97</code> &rarr; fixed <code>6e5a024922</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_peer_max_frame_wmu.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">TLS accept thread blocks on full channel during shutdown</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A / F</span></td>\n      <td>The threaded TLS server (#27552) enqueued each raw conn with a bare blocking <code>ch &lt;- conn</code>. The accept loop, that send, AND the post-loop shutdown (<code>ch.close</code>&rarr;<code>close_idle</code>&rarr;<code>ws.wait</code>) all run on the ONE accept thread, while <code>stop()</code>/<code>close()</code> only flip <code>s.state</code> from another thread. Under a slow-handshake flood (every worker blocked in <code>complete_handshake</code>, channel buffer full of untracked conns) the accept thread blocks on the send, never re-checks <code>s.state</code>, and never reaches <code>close_idle()</code> &mdash; shutdown wedges until a worker handshake times out. Codex P2 on #27552. Fix: enqueue through an interruptible <code>select</code> that polls <code>s.state</code> at <code>accept_poll_timeout</code>, and close the not-yet-queued conn on the accept thread (never idle-tracked &rarr; single close). <strong>Lesson:</strong> when the SAME thread both blocks on a bounded resource AND drives the shutdown wake/cleanup, a full resource self-deadlocks shutdown &mdash; every blocking call on a shutdown-driving thread needs a state-polling escape. <strong>Phase R note:</strong> the regression test PASSED on the buggy commit THREE times (false guards) before it caught the bug &mdash; bare <code>dial_tcp</code> fails the handshake fast (need a valid ClientHello to stall it), and the handshake budget comes from <code>accept_timeout</code> when finite (not <code>tls_handshake_timeout</code>). Always force the timing assert to fail once to read the real value. Commit <code>bf730df187</code> &rarr; fixed <code>e145b0e0c4</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_tls_accept_send_interruptible.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">lenient-parse</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A / D</span></td>\n      <td><code>f.value.int()</code> on <code>:status</code> accepted <code>'200 OK'</code> as 200 and delivered a malformed response as success</td>\n      <td><code>detect_lenient_parse.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">lenient-parse</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A / D</span></td>\n      <td><code>.u64()</code> on <code>content-length</code> accepted <code>'12junk'</code> as 12</td>\n      <td><code>detect_lenient_parse.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">error-scope</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>Stream recv-window violation did <code>return error</code> &rarr; killed the entire connection instead of just that stream</td>\n      <td><code>detect_error_scope.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">state-machine</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>DATA-after-END_STREAM returned a connection error instead of a per-stream RST_STREAM</td>\n      <td>State-machine check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">state-machine</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>DATA frame before HEADERS queued with <code>headers_done = false</code>, delivering status 0 to the caller</td>\n      <td>State-machine check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">sibling-arm</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>SETTINGS arm of dispatch_frame got a <code>!handshaked</code> guard; PING arm in the same match had the identical gap and was never checked</td>\n      <td><code>detect_sibling_arms.ps1</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">event-multiplicity</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>pending_settings_ack bool</code> collapsed N SETTINGS frames into 1 deferred ACK &mdash; RFC 7540 requires one ACK per frame</td>\n      <td>Event-multiplicity check (Angle D)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">transport-safety</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>Reader <code>read()</code> + writer <code>write()</code> share one TLS <code>SSL*</code> which is not full-duplex safe</td>\n      <td>Transport thread-safety (Angle F)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">unbounded-accum</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>pending_ping_acks</code> and <code>pending_settings_acks</code> grew without bound pre-preface &mdash; peer-controlled DoS</td>\n      <td><code>detect_unbounded_accum.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">padding-credit</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>send_window_update(flow_size)</code> wrapped inside <code>if data.len &gt; 0</code>; padding-only DATA frames leaked window permanently</td>\n      <td><code>detect_padding_credit.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">lock-scope TOCTOU</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>peer_max_frame</code> read under <code>fmu</code> to size chunk; <code>fmu</code> released before <code>wmu</code> + DATA write &mdash; reader could shrink limit and ACK in that window, oversizing the frame. Naive fix (<code>fmu&rarr;wmu</code>) deadlocks with <code>do_on_stream</code>'s <code>wmu&rarr;fmu</code> nesting</td>\n      <td><code>detect_fmu_scope.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">cv-liveness</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>Fix returned excess bytes to <code>c.send_window</code> under <code>fmu</code> but omitted <code>fcv.broadcast()</code> &mdash; upload goroutines sleeping in <code>fcv.wait()</code> because the connection window was drained stayed blocked forever; no WINDOW_UPDATE was coming because the bytes were never sent</td>\n      <td><code>detect_missing_fcv_broadcast.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">guard-completeness</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>all_digits(f.value)</code> guard added to <code>:status</code> in <code>H2Conn.read_response</code>, but <code>got_headers = true</code> still fired when the guard rejected the value &mdash; caller received <code>status == 0</code> with no error, a malformed server response silently presented as success</td>\n      <td><code>detect_status_fallthrough.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">HPACK direction</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>SETTINGS_HEADER_TABLE_SIZE</code> from the server constrains our encoder (outgoing request headers), not our decoder; SETTINGS handler called <code>c.decoder.set_max_dynamic_size()</code> &mdash; backwards. A server setting this to 0 silently broke HPACK response decoding when entries were later referenced under a table the decoder had no longer stored</td>\n      <td>HPACK direction check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">body-completeness</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>wait_response</code> returned <code>resp</code> on <code>ended &amp;&amp; got_headers</code> without checking <code>body_so_far == body_expected</code>; a server sending END_STREAM prematurely produced a silent partial body</td>\n      <td>Body completeness check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">1xx informational</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>on_response_headers</code> set <code>s.headers_done = true</code> on any HEADERS frame; a 1xx (100/103) latched <code>got_headers</code> permanently &mdash; the final 200 HEADERS was treated as trailers and silently discarded</td>\n      <td>1xx check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">upload half-close</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>send_body_on_stream</code> returned cleanly on <code>server_ended</code> without RST_STREAM; server kept the stream counted against <code>SETTINGS_MAX_CONCURRENT_STREAMS</code> indefinitely, leaking stream slots</td>\n      <td>Upload half-close check (Angle E)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">wmu&rarr;fmu recheck scope</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>send_body_on_stream</code> wmu&rarr;fmu recheck block handled <code>peer_max_frame</code> reductions but not <code>s.send_window &lt; 0</code>; an INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE reduction between outer <code>fmu.unlock()</code> and inner <code>fmu.lock()</code> drove the stream window negative &rarr; DATA exceeded peer's new window &rarr; FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR</td>\n      <td><code>detect_initial_window_recheck.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">pre-loop TOCTOU</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>send_header_block_locked</code> CONTINUATION loop fixed with per-iteration <code>fmu.lock/read/unlock</code>, but the first multi-frame HEADERS write (before the loop) still used the stale <code>max</code> snapshot from function entry &mdash; same TOCTOU gap on iteration 0</td>\n      <td><code>detect_headers_max_reread.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">single-frame HEADERS TOCTOU</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>send_header_block_locked</code> single-frame early-return (<code>block.len &lt;= max</code>) wrote the HEADERS frame using the function-entry <code>peer_max_frame</code> snapshot with no re-read before the write. Detector previously checked only the second <code>H2HeadersFrame{</code> (multi-frame path), producing a false-negative: the multi-frame re-read was present, so <code>fmu_reads_between == 1</code> &rarr; OK. Updated detector checks both writes independently.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_headers_max_reread.sh</code> (updated)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">server window single-pass</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E / D</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2ServerConn.apply_settings</code> INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE arm assigned <code>c.peer.initial_window_size</code> before the stream loop, then mutated <code>st.send_window</code> and checked for overflow &mdash; single-pass. Overflow on stream N left 1..N-1 inflated and returned PROTOCOL_ERROR instead of FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR. The equivalent fix was applied to <code>H2MuxConn.apply_peer_settings</code> in the same commit but the cross-file sibling was missed.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_server_window_single_pass.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">malformed Content-Length silent-ignore</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2Conn.read_response</code> used <code>all_digits(f.value)</code> as the outer condition on the <code>content-length</code> branch; a non-numeric value silently left <code>has_content_length</code> false, bypassing the body-length check and returning the malformed response as success. Angle A missed it when adding the over-delivery <code>!=</code> fix at line 316: should have traced back to the <code>has_content_length = true</code> setter and asked \"what if the value fails <code>all_digits</code>?\"</td>\n      <td><code>detect_malformed_content_length.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">sync/mux parity: 1xx + END_STREAM</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2Conn.read_response</code> (synchronous client) discarded a 1xx interim HEADERS frame with a bare <code>continue</code>, skipping the <code>if frame.end_stream { break }</code> handling. A 1xx carrying END_STREAM (forbidden, RFC 9113 §8.1) made read_response loop in <code>next_frame()</code> forever, waiting for a final response the stream could no longer send &mdash; a malformed server hangs the request. <strong>The mux path <code>H2MuxConn.on_response_headers</code> already rejected this</strong> (<code>!s.headers_done &amp;&amp; frame.end_stream → reset_stream(.protocol_error)</code>); the sync sibling didn&rsquo;t. Class: the two HTTP/2 client paths (sync + mux) must enforce the same response-validation invariants &mdash; when one adds a check, audit the other. Reviewing this fix surfaced 4 MORE parity gaps in read_response, all fixed in a follow-up parity pass (commit <code>f873315d97</code>): trailers-without-END_STREAM (hang); DATA-before-HEADERS; status <code>&lt;100</code>/<code>&gt;599</code>; <code>101</code> treated as interim (hang). <strong>Process lesson: when a reviewer flags ONE instance of a parity gap between two parallel implementations, audit the WHOLE surface of both</strong> &mdash; Codex flagged 1xx+END_STREAM, but the sync path was missing 5 of the mux path's response-validation invariants. Commits <code>0c61bdd32d</code> + <code>f873315d97</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_h2conn_1xx_end_stream.sh</code> + <code>detect_h2conn_response_parity.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">close_idle Windows double-close</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>close_idle</code> force-closes idle handles with <code>net.close</code> on Windows (to wake a worker blocked in <code>select()</code>, which <code>net.shutdown</code> does not), but the worker&rsquo;s <code>handle_conn</code> defer then closed the SAME fd again via <code>conn.shutdown()</code>. Between the two closes the SOCKET value can be reused by ANY socket the process opens (Windows handle reuse is <strong>process-wide</strong>), so the worker&rsquo;s second close can hit an unrelated socket. <strong>The earlier &ldquo;fix&rdquo; was itself the bug:</strong> a comment claimed the double-close was safe because <code>ch.close()</code> stops the accept loop — but that only reasons about THIS server&rsquo;s accept loop, not process-wide reuse. Codex re-flagged it on #27542. Correct fix: ownership transfer — <code>close_idle</code> records force-closed handles in a <code>closed</code> set (under the lock, Windows-only) and the worker, on <code>was_force_closed(handle)</code>, <strong>relinquishes socket ownership (<code>owns_socket = false</code>) and still calls <code>conn.shutdown()</code></strong> (the first attempt <em>skipped</em> shutdown — see the leak row below), so the fd is closed exactly once. Keep the lock held across the loop: on POSIX the worker stays the sole closer and the held lock orders its close AFTER <code>net.shutdown</code> (dropping the lock reintroduces the original POSIX recycle race). <strong>Lesson:</strong> a &ldquo;safe because &lt;local scope&gt;&rdquo; comment on a process-global resource is a red flag. Commit <code>31405a6150</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_close_idle_double_close.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">close_idle force-close skips TLS cleanup (leak)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>The double-close fix made the worker <strong>skip <code>conn.shutdown()</code> entirely</strong> when <code>was_force_closed</code> was true. But <code>SSLConn.shutdown()</code> does TWO jobs: free the mbedtls TLS resources (SSL ctx, config, certs, RNG, ALPN alloc) AND <code>net.close</code> the socket. Skipping it to avoid the second close also leaked all the TLS resources for every force-closed idle connection at shutdown. Codex caught it on #27542. <strong>The miss:</strong> when gating away a call to suppress ONE of its side effects, enumerate ALL its side effects &mdash; <code>shutdown()</code> conflates socket-close with resource-free. Correct fix: split the concerns &mdash; <code>conn.owns_socket = false</code> (relinquish the socket; <code>shutdown</code> already gates <code>net.close</code> on <code>owns_socket</code>) then call <code>conn.shutdown()</code> so the TLS free always runs. Commit <code>a0f8da73c6</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_close_idle_double_close.sh</code> (updated: requires <code>owns_socket = false</code> relinquish AND unconditional <code>conn.shutdown()</code> &mdash; flags both double-close and leak)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">GOAWAY admission-flag write-race</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A / F</span></td>\n      <td><code>do_on_stream</code> rechecks the terminal admission flags (<code>closed</code>/<code>goaway</code>/<code>shutting_down</code>) under <code>smu</code> <em>while holding <code>wmu</code></em>, then releases <code>smu</code> and writes the request HEADERS still under <code>wmu</code>. The <code>H2GoawayFrame</code> handler set <code>c.goaway</code> under <code>smu</code> <strong>only</strong>, so a GOAWAY landing between that recheck and the HEADERS write let the client open one more stream after observing GOAWAY (RFC 7540 §6.8: &ldquo;Receivers of a GOAWAY frame MUST NOT open additional streams&rdquo;). Angle F enumerated reader-mutable <em>fmu</em>-protected state for earlier TOCTOU fixes but never asked the parallel question for <em>smu</em>-protected admission flags: which terminal-flag <em>setters</em> fail to serialize with the <code>wmu</code> write section that rechecks them? Fix: GOAWAY handler takes <code>wmu</code> before <code>smu</code> so <code>c.goaway</code> can't be set while a writer holds <code>wmu</code> &mdash; the recheck becomes authoritative. Deadlock-free: the reader already takes <code>wmu</code> in the SETTINGS/PING ACK paths (there across a blocking write); the GOAWAY arm only sets a flag. (<code>closed</code> self-heals via transport death; <code>shutting_down</code> via <code>shutdown_when_idle</code> has the same residual gap but is benign.) Commit <code>4496950803</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_goaway_wmu_serialize.sh</code> (Angle F mandatory gate)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">cross-file empty-arm (HPACK table size)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2MuxConn.apply_peer_settings</code> had a comment-only <code>h2_settings_header_table_size</code> arm asserting &ldquo;nothing to constrain&rdquo;; both <code>H2Conn.apply_settings</code> and <code>H2ServerConn.apply_settings</code> set <code>encoder.dyn_table.set_max_size</code> + <code>encoder.pending_max_table_size</code> for the same arm. RFC 7541 &sect;6.3 requires a Dynamic Table Size Update prefix on the next HEADERS block even when the encoder uses only literals. Angle C traced callers/callees but did not compare match arms across sibling functions in parallel files. Fix: add the two-liner under <code>wmu</code> in the mux path. General detector <code>detect_settings_arm_parity.sh</code> now flags any <code>h2_settings_*</code> arm that is comment-only in the reviewed file but has executable code in any sibling <code>h2_*.v</code>. Commit <code>4496950803</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_settings_arm_parity.sh</code> (Angle C mandatory gate)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">HPACK skip on half-closed HEADERS</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>on_headers</code> RST-ed a post-END_STREAM HEADERS frame before decoding its HPACK block. HPACK is connection-wide (RFC 7541 §2.2); skipping a block desyncs the dynamic table &mdash; a subsequent request referencing an entry from that block triggers GOAWAY(COMPRESSION_ERROR). The same PR had the comment &ldquo;the decoder is stateful &mdash; skipping it would desync the dynamic table&rdquo; for the REFUSED_STREAM path and the HPACK-error scope fix (#27569), but missed applying the same reasoning to the half-closed branch in the <strong>same function</strong>. Fix: <code>s.over_end = true</code> + route through <code>on_trailers &rarr; finalize_trailers</code> so decode always runs before RST. <strong>Secondary:</strong> the fix set <code>c.awaiting_cont</code> when END_HEADERS=false; a legal client RST_STREAM then hit the <code>awaiting_cont != 0</code> guard and triggered GOAWAY (connection-kill). Fixed by special-casing RST_STREAM for the awaiting stream in <code>dispatch_frame</code>. Codex P2 on #27589 (review_4588368602). <strong>Lesson: when a correctness rule applies to an event, enumerate ALL handler branches in the function &mdash; Angle E caught the §5.1.2 and HPACK-error branches but missed the half-closed branch three lines away. Secondary: when a fix routes through a path that sets <code>awaiting_cont</code>, audit the guard in <code>dispatch_frame</code> for newly-rejected legitimate frames.</strong> Commit <code>9fe5720b1c</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_hpack_skip_on_half_closed.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">decode-then-discard tracked for DATA only, not HEADERS/CONTINUATION</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>A fix draining in-flight DATA after the server sent RST_STREAM (via a <code>locally_reset</code> map) never asked whether a further HEADERS/CONTINUATION for that SAME now-deleted stream id needed the same treatment &mdash; it hit <code>on_headers</code>'s \"invalid client stream id\" hard error, forcing an unnecessary connection-wide GOAWAY instead of a silent, HPACK-synced drain. The stream struct is gone by RST time (deleted to keep concurrency-limit accounting correct), so the fix needed a connection-level scratch buffer (<code>discard_block</code>), decoded then thrown away, wired into BOTH <code>on_headers</code> and <code>on_continuation</code>. Local 8-angle review had run on this exact commit and returned <code>[]</code> &mdash; the finders checked DATA but never asked \"what about every OTHER frame type that can carry a HEADERS fragment for this id?\" Commit <code>9786c0ac32</code>.</td>\n      <td><em>no detector yet &mdash; see Angle D checklist</em></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">stale trailer_block redecoded on a second invalid HEADERS block</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>on_headers</code>'s existing-stream branch appended a further (invalid, post-response) HEADERS fragment onto <code>existing.trailer_block</code> without clearing it first. If the stream already completed a legitimate trailer section earlier (buffer populated, decoded once, stream lingering because its response was stalled on flow control), the stale already-decoded bytes were decoded a SECOND time along with the new fragment &mdash; reapplying the same HPACK incremental-indexing insertions twice and desyncing the shared dynamic table for every later stream. The inverse failure mode of the half-closed-HEADERS lesson above: decode ran, but on a buffer mixing two logically separate blocks. Fix: clear <code>trailer_block</code> at the start of every fresh top-level HEADERS sequence, not just within one. Commit <code>9786c0ac32</code>.</td>\n      <td><em>no detector yet &mdash; see Angle D checklist</em></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">unsound map-pruning by stream-id ordering</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>The <code>locally_reset</code> tracking map was pruned in <code>on_headers</code> with \"delete every id below the new stream's id,\" reasoning that TCP ordering guarantees lower-numbered streams' frames arrive first. HTTP/2 only guarantees stream IDs are <em>allocated</em> in increasing order &mdash; not that a client transmits all of stream N's frames before opening stream N+2; a client can legally interleave HEADERS(3) ahead of a queued DATA(1) in its own send buffer. Pruning on that false assumption deleted the tracking entry before the genuinely in-flight DATA(1) arrived, reintroducing the exact spurious-double-RST bug the map was built to fix. Fix: removed the pruning heuristic entirely &mdash; the map now only grows, trading a small bounded memory cost for correctness. Commit <code>9786c0ac32</code>.</td>\n      <td><em>no detector yet &mdash; see Angle D checklist</em></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">GOAWAY last_stream_id counted refused/not-yet-processed streams</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>GOAWAY reported <code>last_stream_id</code> bumped in <code>on_headers</code> for ANY syntactically-valid stream id regardless of outcome &mdash; so a stream refused purely for exceeding the concurrency limit (RFC 9113 &sect;5.1.2: REFUSED_STREAM means refused \"prior to any processing,\" already told to the client via its own RST_STREAM as safe-to-retry) inflated GOAWAY's last_stream_id past streams whose outcome was genuinely uncertain. Not Codex-caught &mdash; flagged directly by the human reviewer. Notably, the FIRST fix attempt (bump a new field inside <code>finalize_headers</code>) was itself caught as under-reporting by the SAME local review pass moments later: bumping only at finalize time misses a stream still mid-CONTINUATION when an unrelated error forces a GOAWAY before finalize ever runs for it. Fix: bump at stream-CREATION time in <code>on_headers</code> (the same moment refusal is decided), not at finalize time. Commit <code>fbbfe2c5a1</code>.</td>\n      <td><em>no detector yet &mdash; see Angle D checklist</em></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">orphan CONTINUATION accepted on a locally-reset stream</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>on_continuation</code>'s discard branch checked <code>frame.stream_id in c.locally_reset</code> alone, with no check that a discard block was actually mid-assembly. A standalone CONTINUATION on a stream id reset with a COMPLETE HEADERS block (<code>awaiting_cont</code> never became that id) was silently decoded and discarded instead of a &sect;6.10 connection error. The existing <code>dispatch_frame</code> guard only validated CONTINUATION frames while a sequence WAS in progress (<code>awaiting_cont != 0</code>); it never rejected one when <code>awaiting_cont == 0</code>, so a per-branch fix inside <code>on_continuation</code> alone could not close the gap &mdash; the invariant belongs at the single dispatch-level gate. Codex P2 on #27589 (pullrequestreview-4613845079); a human reviewer additionally caught that the first branch-local fix attempt left the identical gap open for a live (non-reset) stream.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_orphan_continuation_gate.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">unbounded locally_reset tracking map (DoS)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>c.locally_reset[id] = true</code> (8 call sites) had no eviction &mdash; a peer that kept the connection open while repeatedly sending malformed/refused requests grew the map by one entry per stream id forever, even though <code>c.streams</code> itself is bounded to <code>h2_server_max_concurrent_streams</code>. Codex P2 on #27589 (pullrequestreview-4613845079). Fix: <code>mark_locally_reset()</code> helper backed by a <code>locally_reset_order []u32</code> FIFO capped at 128, skipping eviction of the entry currently mid-discard. <strong>Detector false-positive note:</strong> <code>detect_unbounded_accum.sh</code> still flags <code>locally_reset_order</code> as NO-CAP post-fix because the cap constant is named <code>h2_server_max_locally_reset_tracked</code>, not <code>h2_max_*</code> &mdash; its naming-convention grep misses it; confirmed bounded by hand.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_unbounded_accum.sh</code> (enumerate-and-verify; false-negative on naming)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">self-dependent-PRIORITY guard re-RST'd a normally-closed stream</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>The RFC 7540 §5.3.1 self-dependency guard exempted only <code>frame.stream_id in c.locally_reset</code> from re-sending RST_STREAM. A stream that completed NORMALLY (run_request deletes it from <code>c.streams</code> without ever touching <code>locally_reset</code>) was misclassified as still eligible for a fresh RST, sending a non-PRIORITY frame on an already-closed stream (RFC 9113 §5.1) — exactly the violation the exemption existed to avoid, just for the wrong subset of \"closed.\" Codex P2 on #27627 (pullrequestreview-4620220448); same root file as the row above, found in the very next review round after that fix landed. Fix: gate on <code>classify_stream(...) != .closed</code> (reusing the classify_stream generalization from the prior row) instead of the narrower locally_reset-only check — idle/active still get RST (self-dependency is unconditionally a stream error per §5.3.1), any closed stream is left alone regardless of HOW it closed. A pasted external analysis proposed <code>== .idle</code> instead, which is subtly wrong: it would silently tolerate a self-dependent PRIORITY on a currently ACTIVE stream (e.g. one whose response is stalled mid-flow-control), a real protocol violation RFC 7540 §5.3.1 says MUST be a stream error regardless of open/idle state — only CLOSED exempts.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_locally_reset_consumers.sh</code> (enumerate-and-verify)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">refused-stream check masked self-dependency error in finalize_headers</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>finalize_headers</code> checked <code>s.refused</code> (over the concurrency limit, RFC 9113 §5.1.2) BEFORE <code>s.self_dep</code> (RFC 7540 §5.3.1 priority self-dependency), so a HEADERS frame that was BOTH over-limit AND self-dependent answered <code>RST_STREAM(REFUSED_STREAM)</code> instead of <code>PROTOCOL_ERROR</code>. REFUSED_STREAM specifically tells the client the request is safe to retry unchanged elsewhere — but a self-dependent request is malformed independent of the concurrency limit, so retrying it verbatim would just repeat the same error. Codex P2 on #27627 (pullrequestreview-4620412384); found in the very NEXT review round after the sibling classify_stream/self-dep fixes above, in the SAME <code>finalize_headers</code> function those changes were adjacent to but didn't touch. Fix: swap the check order so <code>self_dep</code> is evaluated first. <strong>Lesson: when two independent boolean flags on the same struct are both checked with early-return branches in the same function, the CHECK ORDER encodes an implicit priority between error classes — audit that order whenever a new flag is added alongside an existing one.</strong></td>\n      <td><code>detect_flag_priority_order.sh</code> (enumerate-and-verify)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">classify_stream ignored locally_reset ids never opened via HEADERS</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>classify_stream</code> tested only <code>stream_id &amp; 1 == 1 &amp;&amp; stream_id &lt;= last_stream_id</code> for &ldquo;closed&rdquo;. RFC 9113 &sect;5.1 defines closed as entered when either side sends RST_STREAM, and explicitly permits remembering a reset stream in that state for a bounded window &mdash; exactly what <code>locally_reset</code> already tracks. A self-dependent PRIORITY frame (RFC 7540 &sect;5.3.1) is legal on a stream the client never opened via HEADERS, so it can be <code>locally_reset</code> without ever advancing <code>last_stream_id</code>. Every caller of <code>classify_stream</code> (on_data, handle_control_frame's WINDOW_UPDATE arm, dispatch_frame's RST_STREAM arm) then misclassified later in-flight frames for that id as IDLE, forcing a spurious connection GOAWAY instead of draining them per &sect;6.4. Codex P2 on #27627 (pullrequestreview-4618166175). Fix: <code>classify_stream</code> checks <code>c.locally_reset</code> before the parity/last_stream_id test &mdash; one gate for all three callers rather than three duplicated per-branch checks a future fourth caller could miss.</td>\n      <td>(no detector yet &mdash; regression test <code>test_h2_server_data_after_priority_self_dep_reset_is_drained</code>)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">socket-poll cannot see TLS-buffered app data</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2PooledTransport.read()</code> (<code>h2_pooled_transport.v</code>) called a raw-socket <code>wait_for_read()</code> BEFORE ever calling the wrapped <code>SSLConn.read()</code>. <code>mbedtls_ssl_read()</code>/<code>SSL_read()</code> return at most the caller's buffer size per call; if one underlying socket read pulled in several TLS records at once (routine once a response exceeds <code>h2_conn_read_chunk</code>=16KB), the remainder stays decrypted-and-buffered inside the TLS library with no new socket bytes needed to retrieve it. A raw <code>select()</code>-based poll can't see that &mdash; it only sees the SOCKET's own pending bytes &mdash; so it times out even though <code>conn.read()</code> would return immediately; <code>H2MuxConn.read_loop</code> treats that timeout as a benign wake-up and just retries, so the buffered response is never drained until unrelated new socket data arrives, or never. Codex P1 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4626225521, discussion 3520259789). Fix: delete the outer poll; <code>conn.read()</code> always tries a non-blocking-in-effect decode of buffered data first, falling back to its own internal, now-shortened (200ms&rarr;50ms) bounded WANT_READ wait only when genuinely nothing is buffered &mdash; the shrink keeps a writer's wait for <code>io_mu</code> bounded now that the reader holds it for the whole locked call instead of only when data is already known-ready. <strong>Lesson: a socket-level readiness poll is not equivalent to &ldquo;does the wrapped library have data to return&rdquo; for any transport with its own internal buffering &mdash; such adapters must let the wrapped call drive its own bounded wait instead of gating entry with an outer poll.</strong></td>\n      <td><code>detect_tls_read_precheck_poll.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">close_idle() released a pooled h2 connection's pool reference twice</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>Transport.close_idle()</code> collected every <code>h2_conns</code> entry under <code>t.mu</code> and released each outside the lock, but never removed the entry from the map &mdash; only the connection's own <code>close_transport</code> closure did that, and only once its refcount reached zero. A connection with a genuinely in-flight stream survives ONE release (refcount drops but doesn't hit zero), so it stayed registered; a second <code>close_idle()</code> call (concurrent caller, or simply calling it twice) found the SAME connection again and released it a second time, over-decrementing the refcount below the number of live references &mdash; confirmed directly (<code>refs=0</code> while <code>active_streams=1</code>) rather than inferred through the in-flight fetch's eventual status, since that outcome depends on unrelated timing between the teardown and the reader thread noticing it. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4626225521, discussion 3520259791). Fix: clear <code>h2_conns</code>/<code>h2_dial_id</code> in the SAME locked critical section that collects the entries to release, so no caller (repeat or concurrent) can ever observe &mdash; and therefore re-release &mdash; a connection this call has already claimed. <strong>Lesson: a &ldquo;collect under lock, release outside lock&rdquo; refcounted-pool sweep must remove what it collects from the shared map atomically with the collection, or a connection surviving one release is re-collectible by a repeat sweep.</strong></td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_close_idle_release_is_idempotent</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">dial-failure error code lost across the singleflight boundary</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_dial_failed</code> took the caught error's <code>.msg()</code> string only and reconstructed it via plain <code>error(msg)</code>, discarding <code>.code()</code>. The outer retry loop (<code>request.v</code>'s <code>is_no_need_retry_error</code>) decides whether to give up early on specific codes (<code>net.err_connect_timed_out</code>, <code>net.err_timed_out_code</code>, &hellip;); losing the code made a fundamentally hopeless dial failure retry up to <code>max_retries</code> times instead of failing fast &mdash; a regression from the pre-pooling one-shot h2 path, which propagated the raw <code>IError</code> intact. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4627654418, discussion 3521494715). Fix: thread the caught <code>IError</code> itself through <code>H2DialCall</code> (a new <code>err_code int</code> field alongside the existing <code>err string</code>), reconstructing with <code>error_with_code(msg, code)</code> when <code>code != 0</code>. <strong>Repro:</strong> dialing a closed local port, the pooled path's <code>err.code()</code> was <code>0</code> vs. a raw direct dial's own <code>-68</code> for the identical target on the buggy code; matched after the fix.</td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_dial_failure_preserves_error_code</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">new h2 connection pool didn't inherit Transport.max_idle_conns</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_conns</code> (a new per-origin connection pool added alongside the existing h1 pool in the same PR) had no eviction of its own, so a client touching many distinct h2 origins accumulated one live TLS socket + reader thread per origin forever, ignoring <code>Transport.max_idle_conns</code> &mdash; a limit the h1 pool in the SAME <code>Transport</code> already enforces via <code>checkin</code>'s <code>evict_oldest_locked</code>. This was explicitly documented as a \"known follow-up, out of scope\" in the original PR body rather than a silent oversight, but Codex re-flagged it as P2 on the next round (vlang/v#27643 pullrequestreview-4627654418, discussion 3521494717) and a real fix was requested instead of a further deferral. Fix: <code>evict_idle_h2_if_over_cap</code> &mdash; the h2 pool's analog of the h1 eviction, called from the SAME locked section that registers each new entry, evicting only a currently-IDLE (<code>active_streams==0</code>) connection, least-recently-idle first.</td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_respects_max_idle_conns_cap</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ALPN-probe waiters didn't reuse the h1 pool the dialer just populated</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_await_dial</code> (waiters behind the singleflight ALPN probe) unconditionally called <code>tls_fresh_round_trip</code> &mdash; its OWN fresh dial &mdash; when the probe resolved to http/1.1, even though the dialer (fixed in the prior Codex round of the SAME PR) already completes its own probe connection and checks it into <code>h1_idle</code>. Codex P3 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4627654418, discussion 3521494719). <strong>Why missed:</strong> this is the SAME sibling-handler-scan (adjacent-arm miss) class already in this corpus (classify_stream/self-dep rows above) &mdash; an existing angle, not a coverage gap: the dialer's and the waiter's h1-fallback branches are two arms of the identical ALPN-negotiation decision, and fixing one without re-checking the other repeated a miss pattern this repo has hit at least three times before. Fix: <code>h2_await_dial</code> tries <code>t.checkout(key)</code> first; this only works because the dialer's <code>call.cv.broadcast()</code> also had to move to AFTER its own checkin (previously waiters always raced ahead of the checkin and found nothing to reuse).</td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_waiter_reuses_h1_pool_after_alpn_probe</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">read-timeout watchdog thread lingers for the full deadline after its request completes</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>The first version of the read-timeout-enforcement watchdog (added for the pooled-h2 read_timeout gap) did one bare <code>time.sleep(timeout)</code> then checked the stream. Since <code>req.read_timeout</code> defaults to 30s and EVERY pooled h2 request spawns one, a request completing in milliseconds still left its watchdog thread sleeping up to 30s before waking, finding the stream already done, and exiting &mdash; under sustained throughput this accumulates one lingering thread per recent request. Self-caught during <code>/vreview</code> on the same commit, before push (not an external finding). Fix: poll in bounded ~200ms steps, checking <code>s.ended</code> after each, exiting as soon as the request completes.</td>\n      <td>n/a &mdash; caught reading the new code during review</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">H2PooledTransport.write() reused the shared TLS duration field for its own internal write retry</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>write()</code> called the wrapped <code>ssl.SSLConn</code>'s <code>write()</code>/<code>write_ptr</code> without widening <code>duration</code> first. Both mbedTLS's and OpenSSL's <code>write_ptr</code> reuse that SAME field for their own internal WANT_READ/WANT_WRITE retry loop during the write call &mdash; it is not read-specific at the backend level. <code>new_h2_pooled_transport</code> leaves it at <code>h2_pooled_io_timeout</code> (50ms, needed to bound how long <code>read()</code> holds <code>io_mu</code>), so a peer that merely stalled mid-write (backpressure, not dead) got a ~50ms failure instead of the intended <code>h2_pooled_write_stall_limit</code> (30s). Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4628439062, discussion 3522170277). <strong>Reproduction was the hard part:</strong> client sockets in this codebase are never switched to non-blocking (only the server's threaded-accept path does that), and mbedtls's <code>net_would_block()</code> is hard-coded to return 0 on a blocking POSIX socket &mdash; so <code>MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_WRITE</code> is structurally unreachable via a normal client dial, no amount of payload/timing tuning could reach it. Confirmed by forcing the socket non-blocking + shrinking <code>SO_SNDBUF</code> (both already public APIs) against a captive peer, isolating at the <code>SSLConn.write_ptr</code> level directly: <code>duration=50ms</code> failed at 65ms, <code>duration=2s</code> failed at 2.014s &mdash; the failure latency exactly tracks whatever <code>set_read_timeout()</code> last set. Fix: widen to <code>h2_pooled_write_stall_limit</code> immediately before <code>conn.write()</code> (held inside <code>io_mu</code>, so no race with <code>read()</code>), restore right after, on every exit path.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_pooled_write_duration_reuse.sh</code>; runtime test <code>test_h2_pooled_transport_write_survives_stall_past_io_timeout</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">evict_idle_h2_if_over_cap could evict the connection its own caller just dialed</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>Registering a fresh <code>h2_conns</code> entry and immediately running the over-cap eviction scan in the same locked section meant the brand-new connection (<code>active_streams</code> still 0 &mdash; <code>do_h2</code> hasn't run on it yet) looked idle to its own registration's eviction pass. If it happened to be the only idle candidate found (every other pooled connection busy, or the shared budget consumed entirely by h1 idle entries this function never scans), the loop's first-candidate-wins tie-break picked it, tearing the connection down before its own request ever ran. Codex P1 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4628439062, discussion 3522170273). Fix: <code>evict_idle_h2_if_over_cap(just_registered_key string)</code> &mdash; the scan skips that key explicitly, rather than relying on the (previously believed sufficient, but incorrect once combined with an all-idle map) &ldquo;newest idle_since always loses ties&rdquo; assumption.</td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_does_not_evict_its_own_new_connection</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">read-timeout watchdog armed before the request body finished uploading</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>The read-timeout watchdog (added the round before, see the &ldquo;lingers for the full deadline&rdquo; row above) was spawned in <code>do()</code>, before HEADERS/body ever reached the wire &mdash; so it could fire while still waiting on <code>wmu</code> behind another writer, or mid-upload on a large POST, aborting a request that had not even started waiting for a response yet. <code>req.read_timeout</code> documents itself as the response-read deadline, not an overall request deadline. Codex P1 on vlang/v#27643 (pullrequestreview-4628439062, discussion 3522170275). Fix: moved the <code>spawn</code> into <code>do_on_stream</code>, right before <code>wait_response</code> &mdash; once the request is fully sent and only the response wait remains.</td>\n      <td>runtime test <code>test_mux_read_timeout_excludes_upload_blocked_phase</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">excluding uploads from read_timeout left the upload phase with no timeout bound at all</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>The fix directly above (arm the watchdog only after upload completes, so <code>read_timeout</code> can't fire mid-upload) has a side effect its own Codex finding didn't ask about: <code>send_body_on_stream</code>'s flow-control wait (<code>c.fcv.wait()</code>) has no timeout of its own &mdash; it relies entirely on the connection dying or the watchdog calling <code>wake_send()</code>. With the watchdog now spawned only AFTER upload, a peer that drains to the initial window and then simply never grants another byte of credit (never RSTs, never GOAWAYs, just goes quiet) pins the stream, the pooled connection (<code>active_streams</code> never reaches 0, so it's never idle-evicted either), and the caller's thread &mdash; forever. Self-caught during <code>/vreview</code> on the fix above, before push (not an external finding); found via the Angle C sync-vs-mux parity check: <code>H2Conn.send_body</code> (the one-shot path) waits for window via <code>c.next_frame()!</code>, a blocking read bounded by the connection's own read timeout, so the sync path never had this gap &mdash; only the newly &ldquo;fixed&rdquo; mux path did. Fix: split the single watchdog into two independent phase-scoped calls, each with its own budget &mdash; upload bounded by a NEW, decoupled <code>h2_stream_upload_stall_limit</code> (5min, not tied to <code>req.read_timeout</code> so it can never reintroduce the original bug; <code>-d h2_stream_upload_stall_limit_ms=&lt;n&gt;</code> overridable for a fast test, mirroring <code>mbedtls_client_read_timeout_ms</code>'s <code>$d()</code> pattern), response-wait still bounded by <code>req.read_timeout</code> as before. <strong>Lesson: excluding a phase from a shared timeout to fix a false-positive can silently remove the only bound that phase had &mdash; check what ELSE relied on the timeout you just narrowed before calling the narrowing complete.</strong></td>\n      <td>no detector (timing-shaped, not statically visible); runtime test <code>test_mux_upload_permanently_stalled_eventually_times_out</code> (skips without the <code>-d</code> override so the default suite stays fast)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">evict_idle_h2_if_over_cap's own fix (round 2) was itself incomplete: cap check counted both pools, eviction loop scanned only one</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>Round 2 fixed &ldquo;h2_conns has no eviction at all&rdquo; by adding <code>evict_idle_h2_if_over_cap</code>, whose cap CHECK correctly counted both pools (<code>total_idle_locked() + h2_conns.len &lt;= max_idle_conns</code>) &mdash; but its eviction LOOP only ever scanned <code>h2_conns</code>. With <code>max_idle_conns</code> filled entirely by an idle h1 connection and no other h2 candidate (the new dial's own entry is excluded from candidacy), NEITHER the new h2 connection nor the h1 entry was ever freed, silently exceeding the documented global cap indefinitely. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 round 4 (pullrequestreview-4630174759). Missed by BOTH round 2's and round 3's <code>/vreview</code> passes (round 3 touched the same function to add the just-registered-key exclusion and still didn't re-derive its full contract) &mdash; this is the concrete case for the &ldquo;repeat-offender function&rdquo; rule added to <code>/vreview</code> Phase 0 as a direct result: a function on its second+ touch this branch needs a from-scratch contract re-derivation, not another diff-only scan. Fix: unified the two separate, protocol-scoped evictors into one <code>evict_oldest_idle_locked</code> that scans BOTH pools and evicts whichever is truly oldest-idle. <strong>Sibling instance found by the new detector, open through round 4, fixed round 5:</strong> <code>checkout()</code>'s <code>idle_timeout</code> expiry had no <code>h2_conns</code> equivalent at all &mdash; resolved via a separate <code>H2MuxConn.is_idle_expired</code> check in <code>h2_round_trip</code>'s own fast path (see the round-5 row below), not by adding h2 handling to <code>checkout()</code> itself (which stays h1-only by design).</td>\n      <td><code>detect_pool_cap_scope_symmetry.sh</code>; runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_evicts_h1_idle_to_make_room_for_h2</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">V compiler bug: two same-signature closures in one function conflate their capture context</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>Not a Codex finding &mdash; a genuine V compiler defect found while implementing the round-4 fix for the read-timeout progress-reset. <code>do_on_stream</code> spawns <code>h2_stream_timeout_watchdog</code> twice (upload phase, response phase); adding a second closure argument (<code>activity fn [s] () time.Time {...}</code>) alongside the existing <code>done fn [s] () bool {...}</code> at BOTH call sites meant two pairs of identically-shaped closure literals existed in one enclosing function. The response phase's <code>activity</code> closure silently returned only the connection's INITIAL last-activity snapshot from spawn time, completely defeating the fix, with no compile error and no obviously-wrong behavior short of measuring actual elapsed time against expectation. A <code>voidptr()</code> cast test is a RED HERRING here &mdash; it fails identically for a correctly-working closure (verified via a minimal repro), so it does not distinguish the bug from correct behavior. Diagnosed only via a from-scratch minimal, fully isolated repro (no other test infrastructure to obscure timing) reproducing the exact two-same-signature-closures shape. Fix: pass the captured stream as an explicit parameter to plain, non-capturing NAMED top-level functions instead of closures &mdash; sidesteps the conflation entirely.</td>\n      <td>no detector (V compiler behavior, not project code); manual empirical repro method documented in the Angle A bullet and in memory (v-closure-capture-conflation-bug.md)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">close_idle() cleared h1_idle after collecting it, but never cleared h2_conns &mdash; a second call could re-release the same connection</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>The original <code>close_idle()</code> collected every <code>h2_conns</code> entry under <code>t.mu</code> into a local list for release outside the lock (needed because release can synchronously re-enter <code>t.mu</code>), and correctly called <code>t.h1_idle.clear()</code> right after collecting h1 entries &mdash; but never called the equivalent for <code>h2_conns</code>. A connection with a genuinely in-flight stream survives one <code>release()</code> call (refcount drops but doesn't reach zero), so it stayed registered; a second/concurrent <code>close_idle()</code> call found the SAME connection again and released it a second time, over-decrementing the refcount below the number of live references. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 round 1 (pullrequestreview-4626225521, discussion 3520259791). Found and mechanized this session (2026-07-05), a full round-later, during a full 4-round sweep of #27643 for un-mechanized findings after the user asked why the retrospective had only covered round 4 &mdash; the fix itself landed round 1, but nobody had generalized it into a reusable detector until this sweep. Fix: <code>t.h2_conns.clear()</code>/<code>t.h2_dial_id.clear()</code> in the SAME locked section as the collection.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_pool_collect_release_clear.sh</code>; Phase-R verified exit 1 on <code>6bf4a5934c</code> (pre-fix), exit 0 on <code>339d7ebf19</code> (fixed) and current tree</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">self-referential C connection struct copied by value</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>H2PooledTransport.conn</code> stored the dialed <code>ssl.SSLConn</code> as a bare value field. <code>SSLConn.dial()</code> wires <code>mbedtls_ssl_set_bio(&amp;s.ssl, &amp;s.server_fd, ...)</code> &mdash; a raw C pointer into the STRUCT'S OWN ADDRESS at dial time. <code>H2PooledTransport{conn: conn}</code> bitwise-copied that struct into new memory, so the copy's internal <code>p_bio</code> still pointed at the ORIGINAL dialed object's <code>server_fd</code>, not the copy's own &mdash; every later read/write/close operated on stale/orphaned state instead of the live connection. Codex P1 on vlang/v#27643 round 5 (pullrequestreview-4631763931, discussion 3525390891). <strong>Why missed:</strong> no existing angle asked whether a C-interop wrapper type has internal self-pointers before it gets assigned into a value-typed struct field &mdash; and the correct idiom was already established one file over (<code>H1PooledConn.ssl</code> in transport.v is <code>&amp;ssl.SSLConn</code>), so this was also a sibling-search miss. Fix: <code>conn &amp;ssl.SSLConn</code>; constructor param <code>mut conn &amp;ssl.SSLConn</code> (confirmed by the V compiler that a bare <code>&amp;ssl.SSLConn</code> without <code>mut</code> cannot call mut-receiver methods). <strong>Lesson: any struct wrapping a C-interop connection type whose setup call wires internal pointers to the struct's OWN address must be stored by pointer everywhere it's held for reuse.</strong> Commit <code>22921ba7af</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_pooled_conn_stored_by_value.sh</code>; regression test <code>test_h2_pooled_transport_stores_conn_by_pointer</code> (fails at runtime with a direct pointer-mismatch assertion, independent of the static detector)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">singleflight wake delayed until after unshareable work, head-of-line-blocking waiters</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_dial_and_do</code>'s ALPN-fallback-to-h1 branch didn't wake singleflight waiters until AFTER its own <code>conn.exchange()</code> fully completed and checked the connection in &mdash; a deliberate round-3 choice so a waiter had a real chance to reuse the just-dialed connection. But HTTP/1.1 cannot share one connection across two concurrent requests, so every waiter queued behind however long the DIALER's own response took &mdash; unbounded head-of-line blocking, a regression from ordinary h1 pooling where concurrent first-time callers to a fresh origin dial independently. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 round 5 (pullrequestreview-4631763931, discussion 3525390892). <strong>Why missed:</strong> existing cv-liveness coverage only asks &ldquo;does every path eventually wake waiters&rdquo; &mdash; never &ldquo;is the wake positioned before or after the SPECIFIC work that isn't relevant to what waiters need.&rdquo; A coordinator that does <code>useful_work(); wake()</code> passes every existing liveness check while still serializing unrelated callers behind that work's duration. User confronted the trade-off directly (this reverses a round-3 test's exact invariant) and settled it against RFC 9112 §9.4 (multiple connections exist specifically to avoid this blocking). Fix: wake immediately once the fact waiters need (protocol resolved) is known, before running any of this caller's own unshareable work. No mechanical detector &mdash; &ldquo;is this the earliest point a wake could happen&rdquo; requires tracing what the waiter's own branch depends on.</td>\n      <td>no detector (design/timing judgment call); runtime test <code>test_h2_pool_alpn_fallback_avoids_hol_blocking</code> (timing-based: N concurrent requests must finish in ~1 respond_delay, not ~2)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">singleflight registration TOCTOU across a released-then-reacquired lock</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_round_trip</code> checked <code>t.h2_conns[key]</code> under <code>t.mu</code>, released the lock, then called <code>h2_dial_and_do</code>, which only checked <code>t.dialing[key]</code> before committing to a brand-new singleflight dial. A concurrent dial that finished in the window between those two lock acquisitions was invisible to <code>h2_dial_and_do</code>'s ONE check, causing a redundant TLS/ALPN handshake and orphaning the connection that had just won. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 round 5 (pullrequestreview-4631763931, discussion 3525390895). <strong>Why missed:</strong> the checker (<code>h2_round_trip</code>) and the committer (<code>h2_dial_and_do</code>) are two DIFFERENT functions &mdash; a per-function TOCTOU scan never considered a gap BETWEEN a caller's own check and a callee's separate, later check of the SAME shared state. Fix: <code>h2_dial_and_do</code> rechecks <code>t.h2_conns[key]</code>/<code>t.key_proto[key]</code> under a fresh lock at its own entry before creating a new singleflight coordinator. Also added the sibling fast path's <code>h2_err_retryable_code</code> fallthrough retry, caught via impact analysis during the same fix. <strong>Lesson: when a CHECK and the COMMITTING ACTION live in two different functions (a fast-path caller and a slow-path callee it falls through to), the callee must redo the SAME check under its own fresh lock before committing to expensive/exclusive work.</strong> Commit <code>22921ba7af</code>.</td>\n      <td>no detector (too context-specific to generalize safely); white-box runtime test <code>test_h2_dial_and_do_reuses_pooled_conn_instead_of_redialing</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">caller-callback execution time counted against a network-wait timeout</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td>The h2 pooled-client response watchdog stayed armed while <code>wait_response()</code> ran the caller's <code>on_data</code>/progress callback OUTSIDE the stream lock (deliberately, so a slow callback never stalls delivery to OTHER streams) &mdash; but the watchdog's <code>since()</code> read <code>s.last_activity</code>, a field the callback's own duration never touched, so a callback slower than <code>read_timeout</code> got its stream falsely reset even though nothing was waiting on the network. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27643 round 5 (pullrequestreview-4631763931, discussion 3525390898). <strong>Why missed:</strong> no existing angle asked &ldquo;for a timeout documented as bounding NETWORK wait time, does any non-network activity run during that window, and if so is its duration excluded from the clock.&rdquo; Fix: <code>H2MuxStream.in_callback bool</code>, set around the callback call; <code>h2_response_phase_since</code> returns <code>time.now()</code> (clock paused) while true. <strong>No end-to-end behavioral test is possible here &mdash; verified empirically, don't assume it:</strong> the reader thread updates <code>s.ended</code>/<code>last_activity</code> independent of the callback's progress by design, so any test where the peer delivers frames promptly reaches <code>s.ended == true</code> before the watchdog's poll-after-sleep <code>done()</code> check can ever observe an expired deadline &mdash; the bug is only reachable via genuine flow-control backpressure. Used a direct unit test of the mechanism instead, which fails to even COMPILE on the pre-fix code. Commit <code>22921ba7af</code>.</td>\n      <td>no detector; direct unit test <code>test_h2_response_phase_since_pauses_during_callback</code> (fails to compile on pre-fix code &mdash; a valid Phase-R signal for this class)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">idle-cap check and eviction-scan eligibility mismatch within one pool</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>evict_oldest_idle_locked</code>'s cap check summed <code>t.total_idle_locked() + t.h2_conns.len</code> &mdash; <code>h2_conns.len</code> counts EVERY h2 connection regardless of activity &mdash; but the eviction scan a few lines below only ever considers entries with <code>active_streams == 0</code>. A busy h2 connection inflated the check's total without ever being a candidate the scan could act on, so a pool with busy h2 connections and one genuinely idle h1 connection could look over budget purely from the busy count, wrongly evicting the unrelated idle h1 connection. Codex P3 on vlang/v#27643 round 5 (pullrequestreview-4631763931, discussion 3525390899). <strong>A different dimension of the SAME function's known asymmetry problem</strong> as the round-4 row above: that detector catches a check not referencing BOTH pools; this bug has both pools referenced correctly but doesn't apply the SAME eligibility filter within one of them. Fix: count only <code>active_streams == 0</code> entries, matching the scan's own filter. <strong>My own first fix attempt was itself wrong, caught only by running the full test suite:</strong> excluding the just-registered key from the COUNT too (mirroring candidacy exclusion) broke an existing test &mdash; the just-registered entry still occupies a real pool slot and must be counted, even though it can never be its own eviction victim. Commit <code>22921ba7af</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_idle_cap_counts_busy_conns.sh</code>; regression test <code>test_h2_pool_idle_cap_excludes_busy_h2_conn</code>. Detector catches the reported shape (bare unfiltered <code>.len</code>) but not the follow-up mistake (a filtered count with an extra, wrong exclusion) &mdash; that needs a semantic check not yet built</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">idle-eviction race releases a pooled h2 connection's refcount twice</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">F</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_round_trip</code>'s idle-expiry eviction branch guarded <code>t.h2_conns.delete(key)</code>/<code>t.h2_dial_id.delete(key)</code> with <code>if voidptr(existing) == voidptr(conn)</code> (added in round 5 so a caller that lost the removal race skips the delete), but the following <code>conn.shutdown_when_idle()</code>/<code>conn.release()</code> ran UNCONDITIONALLY. Two concurrent <code>round_trip</code> calls racing the same idle-expired connection &mdash; or a concurrent <code>Transport.close_idle()</code>, which releases every h2 conn unconditionally by design &mdash; both fell through to the ungated <code>release()</code>, over-decrementing <code>H2MuxConn.refs</code> (verified empirically: <code>refs == -1</code> with two racers). <strong>Not a Codex finding</strong> &mdash; found unprompted on a <code>/vreview</code> re-run with zero code changes since round 5, via the rev-16 &ldquo;fresh eyes, no checklist&rdquo; Phase 1 pass: the branch that introduced the gap was itself round 5's own fix for a DIFFERENT finding (idle_timeout not enforced), and neither that round's own A&ndash;G <code>/vreview</code> pass nor Codex caught it. <strong>Why missed the first time:</strong> <code>shutdown_when_idle()</code> sitting right next to <code>release()</code> looks equally safe to call twice &mdash; one genuinely is idempotent, the other decrements a shared refcount &mdash; so a fix that guards the map delete reads as &ldquo;this whole block is now race-safe&rdquo; without checking every statement individually. Fix: compute <code>won_removal := voidptr(existing) == voidptr(conn)</code> once, gate BOTH calls on it, not just the map deletes. Posted as a PR review comment (pullrequestreview-4636271901) BEFORE applying the fix, per explicit user instruction. Commit <code>d8053872bf</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_ungated_release_after_conditional_delete.sh</code> (new); regression test <code>test_h2_pool_idle_evict_race_does_not_double_release</code> (reads <code>t.h2_conns[key]</code> directly via same-module private-field access, asserts <code>pooled.refs == 0</code> after two concurrent racers) &mdash; fails with <code>refs == -1</code> pre-fix, passes after</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">platform-capability gate lost when mirroring a sibling's FUNCTION but not its CALL PATH</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td><code>h2_dial_probe_vschannel</code> (new in vlang/v#27712, Phase 4 vschannel h2 pooling) dialed via <code>new_vschannel_pooled_transport</code>, which unconditionally installs an ALPN wire buffer injected into the handshake as <code>SECBUFFER_APPLICATION_PROTOCOLS</code>. Pre-Windows 8.1 / Server 2012 R2 SChannel has no client-side ALPN and can fail the WHOLE handshake on that buffer &mdash; and the PR's <code>round_trip</code> narrowing put the pooled probe BEFORE the one-shot fallback, so on those systems ordinary HTTPS would fail outright instead of degrading to plain HTTP/1.1. Codex P1 on vlang/v#27712 round 1 (pullrequestreview-4729311285, discussion 3609206168). <strong>Why missed:</strong> the sibling-parity check (Phase 0 step 7, rev 16) RAN &mdash; the adapter's own doc comment says &ldquo;Mirrors vschannel_h2_do's connect sequence&rdquo; &mdash; but the parity diff was scoped to the sibling FUNCTION's body, and the <code>vschannel_alpn_supported() != 0</code> gate lives one level above, in the sibling's caller <code>vschannel_ssl_do</code>: a precondition the call path establishes, invisible to a function-body diff. Fix: gate at the probe (the single funnel for pooled vschannel dials) &mdash; report <code>is_h2: false</code> WITHOUT dialing; the caller memoizes <code>key_proto[key] = 1</code> so every later request short-circuits to the one-shot path, whose own guard dials plain h1 with no ALPN, the exact pre-pooling endpoint. <strong>Repro is STATIC only:</strong> runtime is impossible on Win 11 (<code>alpn_supported()</code> hard-true, unfakeable without hooking ntdll) &mdash; same precedent as <code>detect_mux_requires_closer.sh</code>'s nil case.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_vschannel_pooled_alpn_gate.sh</code> (new) &mdash; exit 1 on reviewed commit <code>e0368d7a2d</code> (verified in both working-tree and [commit] modes), exit 0 after fix commit <code>2ac1373390</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">hand-rolled C matcher weaker than the existing V parser it must agree with (substring vs token)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td><code>http_response_complete</code> (vschannel.c, vlang/v#27714) decided chunk framing via a SUBSTRING match (<code>http_ci_contains(te, \"chunked\")</code>) while the V layer that later decodes the same response (<code>parse_response</code>/<code>has_header_token</code>) matches only real comma-separated transfer-coding tokens. A response with <code>Transfer-Encoding: xchunked</code> whose raw body bytes accidentally look like a complete chunked message (e.g. starting <code>0\\r\\n\\r\\n</code>) made the C read loop stop early while V correctly left the body undecoded &mdash; a silently truncated body. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27714 round 1 (pullrequestreview-4689847198, discussion 3575137170). <strong>Why missed:</strong> the sibling-parity check looks for siblings in the same file/module family &mdash; the authoritative sibling here was in a DIFFERENT LANGUAGE AND LAYER (V's <code>has_header_token</code>), and nothing prompted a cross-layer semantic-agreement diff for two interpretations of the same wire field. Fix: <code>http_has_transfer_coding</code> mirrors <code>has_header_token</code> exactly (split on commas, strip <code>;params</code>, trim OWS, ci-compare whole tokens). Commit <code>a58f706b36</code>.</td>\n      <td>no static detector (deliberate: in-repo runtime regression test is stronger); <code>test_vschannel_h1_transfer_encoding_extension_token_is_not_chunked</code> &mdash; body truncated to the 5-byte fake terminator on pre-fix code, full raw body after</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">conservative-fallback design silently reverts to pre-fix behavior on legal input variants (trailing OWS)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>http_find_header</code> (vschannel.c, vlang/v#27714) trimmed only LEADING spaces/tabs from header values, so the grammar-legal <code>Content-Length: 27&nbsp;</code> (RFC 7230 allows OWS on both sides of a field value) failed the deliberately-strict digit parse &mdash; and the design's safe fallback (&ldquo;anything unparseable &rarr; read until close&rdquo;) quietly reinstated the exact keep-alive hang the whole feature exists to fix, for any server emitting trailing whitespace. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27714 round 1 (pullrequestreview-4689847198, discussion 3575137175). <strong>Why missed:</strong> the strict-parse-with-safe-fallback shape reads as &ldquo;can't be wrong, worst case is the old behavior&rdquo; &mdash; which frames spurious fallbacks as harmless exactly when the old behavior IS the bug; and testing used only canonical header formatting, never legal variants. Fix: trim trailing OWS in the shared extractor, so both consumers (Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding) benefit. Commit <code>a58f706b36</code>.</td>\n      <td>no static detector (deliberate: runtime regression test); <code>test_vschannel_h1_content_length_with_trailing_whitespace_still_frames</code> &mdash; waited out the peer's full 3s close on pre-fix code (3.054s measured), completes &lt;1.5s after</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">behavior change verified only in its own module; sibling-module consumer tests broke (one masked by the flaky list)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#27712's <code>round_trip</code> narrowing makes an h2-enabled https request to an h1-only origin open TWO connections on native Windows (ALPN probe + one-shot dial). Verification ran <code>vlib/net/http/</code> in both configs plus smoke tests &mdash; but single-accept https test servers CONSUMING <code>http.fetch</code> live in a SIBLING module: <code>mbedtls_head_with_content_length_test.v</code> failed on all 3 Windows CI jobs (probe consumed the one accept slot &rarr; deferred <code>listener.shutdown()</code> &rarr; real request ECONNREFUSED), and <code>mbedtls_concurrent_large_response_test.v</code> failed identically but was MASKED on CI by the known-flaky-file list (<code>VTEST_FAIL_FLAKY=0</code> swallows it after 3 retries) &mdash; locally 3/3 deterministic. Found by CI triage, not review: 8 of 9 failing jobs that day WERE master's own breakage, and only per-job failure-signature comparison against master's own runs caught the one PR-caused failure hiding among them. <strong>Second-order miss in the fix itself:</strong> the probe's close is observed server-side as a CLEAN 0-byte read (close_notify EOF), NOT a read error &mdash; an error-only probe-skip let the chunked server write its entire response into the dead probe connection; probe detection must be <code>n &lt;= 0</code>. <strong>Why missed:</strong> present-but-not-run &mdash; the &ldquo;run net consumers for client changes&rdquo; rule already existed (AGENTS.md workflow memory) but the sweep only covered the changed module. Fix commit <code>5c81eb79b8</code>.</td>\n      <td>no detector; mechanical sweep instead: files matching <code>new_ssl_listener</code> across ALL of vlib that also reference <code>fetch(</code>/<code>get_text(</code>/<code>req.do()</code>, each run on native Windows &mdash; and any &ldquo;*FAILURE* of the known flaky test file &hellip; is ignored&rdquo; line in a green run is a failure to READ, not noise</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ecdsa key leak: missing <code>.free()</code> on a new test's PrivateKey/PublicKey</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>tls13_client_hello_test.v</code>'s <code>test_build_client_hello_structure</code> called <code>ecdsa.PrivateKey.new()!</code> and <code>priv.public_key()!</code> but never <code>.free()</code>'d either &mdash; confirmed via ASan LeakSanitizer on vlang/v#27680's <code>sanitize-address-clang</code> CI job (4232 bytes / 92 allocations leaked, top frame <code>CRYPTO_zalloc</code> &larr; <code>crypto__ecdsa__PrivateKey__static__new</code>). Every OTHER ecdsa test (<code>ecdsa_test.v</code>, <code>util_test.v</code>, <code>ecdsa_p256_ecdh_test.v</code>) and every other net.quic test using ecdsa keys (<code>tls13_handshake_test.v</code>, <code>tls13_certificate_chain_test.v</code>) already frees both &mdash; this was the one place a brand-new test skipped the established, universally-followed convention. Not a Codex finding &mdash; found by the user directly (&ldquo;Memory leak and heap-buffer overflow should have been caught during /vreview&rdquo;) after CI sanitizer jobs surfaced it; no <code>/vreview</code> pass had run a mechanical check for this bug class before. <strong>Why missed:</strong> no existing angle asked &ldquo;does this new test's C-interop resource constructor have a matching <code>.free()</code> anywhere in the file&rdquo; &mdash; Angle A/G's <code>unsafe{}</code>/C-interop bullets cover pointer lifetime and self-referential structs, not a plain missing-finalizer leak on a type whose OWN module doc says &ldquo;Dont forget to call .free() after finish with your key.&rdquo; Fix: <code>defer { priv.free() }</code> / <code>defer { pub_key.free() }</code> right after each constructor call &mdash; verified <code>public_key()</code> uses <code>EVP_PKEY_dup</code> (a real duplicate, not a shared pointer), so freeing both is not a double-free. Commit <code>2a51a146b4</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_ecdsa_key_missing_free.sh vlib/net/quic/tls13_client_hello_test.v 8084407c30</code> &mdash; exit 1 (LEAKED at lines 162/163); exit 0 on the fixed tree</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">mbedtls PEM parse: <code>.bytes()</code>-derived buffer given a manual <code>+1</code> NUL-terminator length</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>x509_standalone.v</code>'s <code>verify_certificate_chain</code> AND its own test <code>x509_standalone_test.v</code> both did <code>pem := &lt;string&gt;.bytes()</code> then passed <code>usize(pem.len + 1)</code> to <code>mbedtls_x509_crt_parse</code> &mdash; mbedTLS's PEM-vs-DER sniff requires a REAL trailing NUL byte (<code>buf[buflen-1]=='\\0'</code>), but <code>.bytes()</code> allocates a fresh <code>[]u8</code> of EXACTLY <code>.len</code> bytes with no such byte, so the <code>+1</code> reads one byte past the heap allocation. Confirmed via ASan on vlang/v#27680's <code>sanitize-address-clang-without-gc</code> CI job: <code>heap-buffer-overflow ... in mbedtls_x509_crt_parse</code>, a 1-byte READ exactly 0 bytes past an allocation whose size matched the source string's length. The pre-existing, correct sibling pattern in the SAME module (<code>ssl_connection.c.v</code>'s <code>new_sslcerts_in_memory_with_rng</code>, 4 call sites) passes <code>&lt;string&gt;.str</code> instead of <code>.bytes()</code> &mdash; this new file duplicated the mistake in BOTH its production function and its own test, never checking the sibling. Not a Codex finding &mdash; same user report as the row above. <strong>Why missed:</strong> Phase 0 step 7 (&ldquo;find the closest existing sibling ... diff intent line-by-line&rdquo;) calls for exactly this comparison but wasn't run against <code>ssl_connection.c.v</code>'s existing PEM-parsing call sites when this NEW file was first written; the file's own doc comment even discusses the DER path's NUL-terminator rules at length (for <code>build_certificate_chain</code>) without cross-checking the PEM path two functions below it against the module's own established convention. Fix: use <code>&lt;string&gt;.str</code> directly in both files. Commit <code>2a51a146b4</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_bytes_nul_terminator_mismatch.sh vlib/net/mbedtls/x509_standalone.v 60d5c3e5b4</code> &mdash; exit 1 (MISMATCH at line 177); exit 0 on the fixed tree; also verified against the correct sibling <code>ssl_connection.c.v</code> (0 false positives)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">certificate hostname/SAN never verified &mdash; any trusted-CA cert for any host passes</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>net.mbedtls.verify_certificate_chain</code> always passed <code>cn: nil</code> to <code>mbedtls_x509_crt_verify</code> &mdash; no caller anywhere in <code>net.quic</code> had a hostname to give it, since the standalone design (no <code>mbedtls_ssl_context</code>) never threaded the SNI name through. Chain-of-trust validation succeeded for ANY certificate signed by a trusted CA regardless of which host it was actually issued for &mdash; a hostname-impersonation hole. Codex P1 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4781706846). <strong>Why missed:</strong> Angle G1 already checks &ldquo;is the cert validation callback non-null&rdquo; and &ldquo;is ALPN complete&rdquo; &mdash; but its detector is keyed on <code>SslConfig{}</code>/<code>ssl_config</code> construction, which this hand-rolled standalone path deliberately never does. The angle existed; the grep target didn't match the new shape. Fix: <code>verify_certificate_chain(chain, ca_bundle_pem, hostname string)</code>, threaded from <code>Tls13ClientHandshake.server_name</code> (a field that didn't even exist on the handshake struct until this fix &mdash; it was only ever passed transiently into ClientHello construction and then discarded); empty hostname passed through as an empty C string (fails closed) rather than special-cased to &ldquo;skip&rdquo;. New Angle G8 bullet in SKILL.md generalizing this: for any hand-rolled/standalone crypto path, run G1's checklist BY HAND since its detector can't see it.</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_verify_certificate_chain_rejects_hostname_mismatch</code> asserts <code>MBEDTLS_X509_BADCERT_CN_MISMATCH (0x04)</code> now appears in the verify flags for a wrong hostname; reproduced the gap first via a standalone script calling the raw C API with <code>cn: nil</code>, confirming the flag never sets regardless of hostname</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ClientHello never sends ALPN &mdash; mandatory for QUIC</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 9001 §8.1 makes ALPN mandatory for QUIC (no other application-protocol negotiation mechanism exists); this ClientHello never encoded the extension at all, so a real HTTP/3 server would terminate the handshake with <code>no_application_protocol</code> instead of completing. Codex P1 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4781706846). <strong>Why missed:</strong> same G1-detector-blind-spot as the row above (its ALPN check is keyed on <code>SslConfig{}</code>); additionally, a MISSING mandatory extension produces no diff line for any diff-driven angle to react to &mdash; there was nothing wrong to spot, only something absent. Fix: <code>encode_alpn_extension</code>/<code>decode_alpn_response</code> (RFC 7301), <code>ClientHelloParams.alpn_protocols</code> made required (empty list rejected), server's single-entry selection validated against what was offered, new <code>TlsAlert.no_application_protocol = 120</code>. <strong>Lesson: for any new protocol/handshake integration, write the spec's own MUST-send-this-extension list as a checklist BEFORE reading the diff (Angle E's H2 conformance-matrix technique, generalized beyond HTTP/2) &mdash; an absent requirement is invisible to diff-driven review by construction.</strong></td>\n      <td>4 new tests: rejects empty <code>alpn_protocols</code> at encode time; <code>process_encrypted_extensions</code> rejects a missing ALPN extension and an unoffered server selection, both asserting the exact <code>no_application_protocol</code> QUIC error code</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">transport-parameter connection-ID authentication gap (RFC 9000 §7.3)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>process_encrypted_extensions</code> validated only <code>initial_source_connection_id</code> against the observed packet-header SCID; it never checked <code>original_destination_connection_id</code> (the client's own first-Initial DCID) or <code>retry_source_connection_id</code> (the Retry packet's SCID, when one occurred) &mdash; RFC 9000 §7.3's own anti-tampering mechanism for detecting an off-path attacker that injected a spoofed Retry. Codex P2 on #27680. Fix: both validated (missing or mismatched &rarr; TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR); <code>retry_source_connection_id</code> present with no Retry having occurred is also rejected (RFC 9000 §7.3's other direction).</td>\n      <td>4 new tests covering: missing/mismatched <code>original_destination_connection_id</code>, missing/mismatched <code>retry_source_connection_id</code> when a Retry occurred, and present-with-no-Retry</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">transport-parameter failures mapped to the wrong CONNECTION_CLOSE error code</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Every <code>decode_transport_parameters</code> failure &mdash; structural AND its own PRE-EXISTING §18.2 bound violations (<code>ack_delay_exponent</code>, <code>max_udp_payload_size</code>, etc.) &mdash; was routed through a TLS <code>decode_error</code> alert (&rarr; QUIC code 0x132) instead of QUIC's native <code>TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR</code> (0x08, RFC 9000 §20.1), which isn't a TLS alert at all. Codex P2 on #27680. Fix: new <code>transport_parameter_error()</code> helper that bypasses the <code>TlsAlert</code>/0x100+alert mapping entirely, used for every failure in this function (not just the two new connection-ID checks above).</td>\n      <td>all connection-ID and ALPN regression tests above assert <code>err.code()</code> equals the specific expected QUIC error code, not just that SOME error occurred</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">non-minimal QUIC varint encoding wrongly rejected on decode</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>decode_varint</code> rejected any value encoded in a longer-than-minimal length class &mdash; but RFC 9000 §16 permits (does not require rejecting) any of the 4 length classes a value fits in; only the ENCODER is expected to prefer the minimal form. A fully compliant peer choosing a longer form for any reason could never complete a handshake, since this function backs packet lengths, tokens, transport parameters, and ACK ranges alike. Codex P2 on #27680. <strong>Why missed:</strong> the removed code's OWN doc comment asserted &ldquo;RFC 9000 §16 requires the smallest possible encoding&rdquo; as fact, without citing the exact clause &mdash; an unverified claim about spec text, later disproved by fetching the actual RFC. Fix: removed the rejection (encode side unaffected; it already always produces minimal output).</td>\n      <td>4 new tests decode the same value (5) across all 3 non-minimal length classes and assert success, not rejection</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">short-header spin_bit/key_phase always decoded as false</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>parse_short_header</code> returned <code>QuicShortHeader{dcid: dcid}</code> &mdash; a struct literal that only sets ONE of its 3 fields, silently leaving <code>spin_bit</code>/<code>key_phase</code> at their zero-value <code>false</code> regardless of the actual wire bits. A caller relying on <code>key_phase</code> to detect a peer-initiated key update (RFC 9001 §6) could never see the rotation. Codex P2 on #27680. <strong>Why missed:</strong> a struct literal that omits fields is syntactically valid V and compiles clean &mdash; nothing flags \"did you mean to set every field this struct declares\" without reading the struct definition alongside every literal constructing it.</td>\n      <td>new test asserts both bits independently and together against hand-crafted first-byte values</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">QUIC v1's 20-byte connection-ID limit unenforced on both encode and parse</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Both <code>encode_long_header</code> and <code>parse_long_header</code> only enforced the WIRE FORMAT's own 255-byte length-field limit (a single length byte can represent 0-255), not QUIC v1's OWN protocol limit of 20 bytes (RFC 9000 §17.2: \"MUST NOT exceed 20 bytes\") &mdash; a 21-255 byte connection ID encoded/parsed successfully despite being spec-invalid for v1. Codex P2 on #27680. Fix: new <code>quic_v1_max_cid_len = 20</code> constant enforced on both paths (encode conditional on <code>h.version == quic_v1</code> since the struct is version-generic; parse unconditional since non-v1 versions are now rejected earlier in the same function, see next row).</td>\n      <td>2 new tests: encode rejects a 21-byte v1 DCID; parse rejects a hand-built header with <code>dcid_len = 21</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">long-header packet-type decoding not version-gated</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>peek_long_header_type</code> applied QUIC v1's type-bit mapping (RFC 9000 §17.2) to ANY version field &mdash; but QUIC v2 (RFC 9369 §3.2) deliberately assigns DIFFERENT meanings to the same two bits, so a v2 Initial packet could be misclassified as v1's zero_rtt, steering the caller toward the wrong packet-number space and keys. Codex P2 on #27680. Fix: <code>parse_long_header</code> now rejects any non-v1 nonzero version outright, before ever calling the type decoder &mdash; v2 (and any future version) is out of this client's committed scope entirely, so guessing at its bit layout was never correct to attempt.</td>\n      <td>new test hand-builds a header with QUIC v2's registered version value (0x6b3343cf) and asserts rejection</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">doc comment stated the OPPOSITE of RFC 9001 §5.2's Retry re-derivation rule</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>derive_initial_secrets</code>'s doc comment claimed Initial secrets are derived ONCE from the client's original DCID and never re-derived after a Retry. RFC 9001 §5.2's actual text: \"The secrets used for constructing subsequent Initial packets change when a server sends a Retry packet to use the connection ID value selected by the server\" &mdash; precisely the opposite. Codex P2 on #27680; verified via a live fetch of the RFC text before touching anything, since this was a direct factual disagreement between the existing comment and the reviewer, and neither side should be trusted from memory alone. No code bug (the function itself is DCID-agnostic and already correct) but the wrong doc comment would have misdirected Phase 9's real <code>QuicConn</code> implementation into deriving the wrong keys after every Retry &mdash; a bug that hadn't been written yet, caught before it could be. Fix: rewrote the comment with the verified RFC quote, and explicitly distinguished this from the SEPARATE, unrelated <code>original_destination_connection_id</code> transport parameter (RFC 9000 §7.3 authentication, not secret derivation) &mdash; the likely source of the original conflation.</td>\n      <td>none (doc-only); existing test <code>test_derive_initial_secrets_is_sensitive_to_which_dcid_is_passed</code> already exercised the CORRECT behavior and needed no change &mdash; only the comment was wrong</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">missing 2^60 stream-count limit (RFC 9000 §4.6)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Neither <code>initial_max_streams_bidi</code> nor <code>initial_max_streams_uni</code> were bounds-checked on decode; RFC 9000 §4.6 requires TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR above 2^60 since a stream ID is the count times 4 (2 bits of type/initiator per ID), so a larger count risks reaching or exceeding the 62-bit varint space. Codex P2 on #27680. Fix: <code>max_initial_max_streams = 1u64 &lt;&lt; 60</code>, checked on decode (mapped to TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR per the row above) and mirrored on encode.</td>\n      <td>4 new tests: reject/accept-boundary pairs on decode for both parameters, plus an encode-side rejection test</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">encode-side transport-parameter bounds entirely absent (asymmetric with decode)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>encode_transport_parameters</code> had ZERO bounds validation on any field, even though <code>decode_transport_parameters</code> already enforced <code>max_udp_payload_size &gt;= 1200</code>, <code>ack_delay_exponent &lt;= 20</code>, <code>max_ack_delay &lt; 2^14</code>, <code>active_connection_id_limit &gt;= 2</code> &mdash; and <code>build_client_hello</code> forwards this struct unnormalized, so a caller configuration error silently produced an invalid ClientHello nobody would catch until a real server rejected it. Codex P2 on #27680. <strong>Lesson generalizing beyond this PR, mirroring this corpus's own \"server INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE single-pass\" and sync/mux-parity rows: when a decode/parse function enforces a bound, grep for the paired encode/build function on the SAME field &mdash; an asymmetric pair (checked one direction only) is exactly this bug's shape, whether the \"two sides\" are two files or, as here, two directions of the same data.</strong> Fix: every decode-side bound now mirrored on encode.</td>\n      <td>4 new tests, one per bound, asserting <code>encode_transport_parameters</code> now rejects the identical out-of-range values <code>decode_transport_parameters</code> already rejected</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">Fixed Bit never checked on either header parser (RFC 9000 §17.2/§17.3.1)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Neither <code>parse_long_header</code> nor <code>parse_short_header</code> checked the Fixed Bit (0x40): \"Packets containing a zero value for this bit are not valid packets in this version and MUST be discarded.\" A packet with Header Form set but Fixed Bit clear was silently parsed as an ordinary long/short header instead of rejected. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4782360314) &mdash; caught AFTER a full-file holistic <code>/vreview</code> pass and this PR's own new Angle G8 had already run on this exact file. <strong>Lesson: an ad-hoc \"write every MUST as a checklist before reading the diff\" pass is recall-dependent and can still miss a one-sentence MUST mid-review; this is the finding that drove building <code>quic_conformance_matrix.md</code> as a standing artifact instead.</strong> Fix: both parsers now reject a clear Fixed Bit immediately after the Version Negotiation / top-bit checks.</td>\n      <td>none (new): <code>test_long_header_rejects_clear_fixed_bit</code>, <code>test_short_header_rejects_clear_fixed_bit</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">preferred_address connection_id length silently wraps on encode (u8 cast)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>encode_preferred_address</code> cast <code>pa.connection_id.len</code> straight to a single length byte with no bounds check &mdash; a connection ID of 256 bytes wraps to a length byte of 0, corrupting the wire encoding for any decoder (length byte says 0, but 256 bytes of connection-ID data follow, desyncing the fixed 16-byte stateless_reset_token that comes after). Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4782360314), same round as the Fixed Bit row above. Matches this corpus's existing G3 pattern (wire-integer overflow via silent narrowing cast) exactly, just on preferred_address instead of a length/count field elsewhere. Fix: reject <code>connection_id.len &gt; 255</code> before the cast, mirroring <code>encode_long_header</code>'s identical existing 255-byte connection-ID check.</td>\n      <td>none (new): <code>test_encode_preferred_address_rejects_overlong_connection_id</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">client ClientHello never sends its own mandatory initial_source_connection_id (RFC 9000 §7.3)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>build_client_hello</code> validated that four SERVER-only transport parameters were ABSENT from the client's own ClientHello, but never validated that <code>initial_source_connection_id</code> &mdash; mandatory on BOTH sides per RFC 9000 §7.3 (\"An endpoint MUST treat absence of the initial_source_connection_id transport parameter from either endpoint... as a connection error\") &mdash; was actually PRESENT. Every ClientHello this code could produce omitted it; confirmed via grep that no test or production call site ever populated it on the client-send path. <strong>Not Codex-found</strong> &mdash; surfaced by this session's own construction of <code>quic_conformance_matrix.md</code> (walking RFC 9000 §7.3 row-by-row against the actual client-send code path), the exact same way the H2 matrix's founding audit (2026-06-27) found gaps G1/G2/G4 no external review had flagged. Phase-R reproduced on the buggy code first (a scratch script confirmed <code>build_client_hello</code> silently succeeded with the field absent) before fixing. Fix: same presence-check pattern as the four existing absence-checks; 7 existing test call sites updated to supply a real value.</td>\n      <td>none (new): <code>test_build_client_hello_rejects_missing_initial_source_connection_id</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">leaf certificate KeyUsage/ExtendedKeyUsage never checked (P1)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>verify_certificate_chain</code>/<code>verify_server_certificate_chain</code> checked chain-of-trust and hostname only &mdash; a certificate restricted to a non-server purpose (clientAuth-only EKU, or KeyUsage lacking digitalSignature) could still authenticate a QUIC server. A real TLS handshake separately calls <code>mbedtls_ssl_check_cert_usage</code>; this standalone (never-constructs-an-SSL-context) path had no equivalent. Codex P1 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111) &mdash; the highest-severity finding on this PR to date. Fixed: new <code>net.mbedtls.check_server_cert_usage</code>, a thin C shim delegating to mbedTLS's own UNMODIFIED <code>mbedtls_x509_crt_check_key_usage</code>/<code>check_extended_key_usage</code>, called right after chain+hostname verification succeeds; both already implement X.509's \"absent extension means unrestricted\" rule, so existing unrestricted test certs aren't over-rejected. <strong>Honest test-coverage limit, stated rather than glossed over: this repo has no certificate fixture with a restrictive KeyUsage/EKU, so the actual REJECTION path isn't proven with a real certificate</strong> &mdash; coverage is the positive path only. New Angle G8 checklist item (d).</td>\n      <td>none (new): <code>test_check_server_cert_usage_accepts_existing_test_certs</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">CertificateVerify doesn't check the certificate key is the curve the SignatureScheme claims</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>mbedtls_pk_verify_ext</code> only confirms the key is SOME EC key; <code>ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256</code> (RFC 8446 §4.2.3) names ONE specific curve (P-256). A P-384/P-521 certificate's key could be accepted under that scheme name even though its signature verifies correctly on its OWN curve &mdash; a protocol-identity mismatch, not broken crypto. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111), same round as the P1 above. Fixed: new <code>net.mbedtls.public_key_curve_is_secp256r1</code> (a C shim resolving <code>mbedtls_pk_ec</code>'s unusual by-value argument, then <code>mbedtls_ecp_keypair_get_group_id</code>), checked in the ecdsa branch before verification. Same fixture caveat as the P1 above: no non-P256 EC certificate exists in this repo; proven via the closest available adjacent case instead (an RSA key must also fail this check, and does). New Angle G8 checklist item (e).</td>\n      <td>none (new): <code>test_public_key_curve_is_secp256r1_true</code>, <code>test_verify_certificate_verify_signature_rejects_wrong_key_type_for_ecdsa</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">preferred_address connection ID missing QUIC v1's 20-byte limit (only the 255-byte wire limit was checked)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>decode_preferred_address</code>/<code>encode_preferred_address</code> enforced the wire format's own 255-byte single-length-byte limit but never QUIC v1's SEPARATE 20-byte connection-ID limit (RFC 9000 §17.2) &mdash; the same limit header.v's dcid/scid already enforce. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111).</td>\n      <td>2 new tests: <code>test_encode_preferred_address_rejects_v1_cid_over_20_bytes</code>, <code>test_decode_preferred_address_rejects_v1_cid_over_20_bytes</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">sibling connection-ID transport parameters missing the SAME 20-byte limit</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><strong>Found by this session's OWN sibling sweep, not Codex</strong> &mdash; after fixing the preferred_address instance above, auditing the other connection-ID-carrying fields in the SAME file (<code>initial_source_connection_id</code>, <code>original_destination_connection_id</code>, <code>retry_source_connection_id</code> &mdash; each defined as an echo of a wire DCID/SCID already bound by the identical limit, RFC 9000 §7.3, verified via a live RFC fetch) found NONE of them had the check either, on either encode or decode. Closed proactively in the same session rather than waiting for a fifth Codex round to find the 2nd/3rd/4th instances one at a time.</td>\n      <td>6 new tests, one per field per direction</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">encode_long_header doesn't reject unsupported versions before encoding type bits</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><code>parse_long_header</code> already rejects any non-v1 version outright; <code>encode_long_header</code> had no equivalent, so a caller constructing a non-v1 <code>QuicLongHeader</code> (nothing in the type system prevents it) would get <code>h.typ</code> silently mis-encoded under v1's OWN bit mapping &mdash; meaningless/wrong for a version (e.g. QUIC v2, RFC 9369 §3.2) that assigns those bits differently. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111).</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_encode_long_header_rejects_unsupported_version</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">parse_encrypted_extensions only rejected early_data, not other EE-illegal extensions</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 8446 §4.2: \"Implementations MUST NOT send extension responses if the remote endpoint did not send the corresponding extension requests... MUST abort... with an unsupported_extension alert.\" <code>key_share</code>/<code>supported_versions</code> (ClientHello/ServerHello/HRR-only per §4.2's own per-message table, illegal in EncryptedExtensions regardless of what was offered) passed through unrejected. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111). Fixed: explicit allowlist (server_name, supported_groups, alpn, quic_transport_parameters); the caller (<code>process_encrypted_extensions</code>) also fixed to preserve a specific nonzero error code instead of unconditionally remapping every parse failure to <code>decode_error</code>, so this now correctly maps to QUIC's <code>unsupported_extension</code> (110) CONNECTION_CLOSE code.</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_parse_encrypted_extensions_rejects_key_share</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">parse_version_negotiation accepts an empty versions list</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>A VN packet with nothing after the SCID has <code>remaining == 0</code>, which trivially passes the <code>remaining % 4 != 0</code> check, so the parser returned a <code>QuicVersionNegotiation</code> with an empty <code>versions</code> list instead of rejecting it &mdash; a VN packet exists specifically to advertise supported versions; one listing zero is not a meaningful offer. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111). Matches this codebase's own established convention of rejecting other degenerate-empty structures (empty ALPN list, empty certificate list).</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_version_negotiation_rejects_empty_versions_list</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">PROGRESS.md still contradicted the already-corrected RFC 9001 §5.2 Retry re-derivation doc fix</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>A PREVIOUS round (rev 23 row above) fixed <code>initial_secrets.v</code>'s doc comment, which had wrongly claimed Initial secrets are derived once and never re-derived after a Retry &mdash; but never checked whether the SAME wrong claim existed anywhere else in the repo. <code>PROGRESS.md</code> had a second copy of it. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4783410111). <strong>Lesson: fixing one copy of a stated fact is not the same as grepping for every other copy of it.</strong></td>\n      <td>none (doc-only)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ClientHello never advertised signature_algorithms_cert (P1)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 8446 §4.2.3: certificate signatures can use a broader scheme set than CertificateVerify, advertised separately via signature_algorithms_cert. This client accepts RSA-PKCS1v1.5-signed certificates (mbedTLS's generic X.509 verifier already supports them) but never said so &mdash; a server whose chain uses that common algorithm had no matching entry to select against and could refuse the chain entirely. Codex P1 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664), the highest-severity finding so far. Fixed: new extension with the 4 existing schemes plus rsa_pkcs1_sha256/384/512; signature_algorithms (CertificateVerify) unchanged and still strict.</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_encode_signature_algorithms_cert_extension_wire_format</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ServerHello/HelloRetryRequest had no extension allowlist</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td><strong>The exact same class of gap the EncryptedExtensions allowlist fix (rev 23/24) closed, never extended to the sibling message types.</strong> An unsolicited extension (e.g. alpn, EncryptedExtensions-only per RFC 8446 §4.2's own per-message table) passed through both branches silently ignored instead of the mandatory unsupported_extension abort. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664). Fixed: variant-specific allowlists (ServerHello: supported_versions, key_share; HRR: + cookie) via a shared helper, plus the same error-code-preservation fix in the caller.</td>\n      <td>2 new tests: <code>test_parse_server_hello_rejects_unsolicited_alpn</code>, <code>test_parse_server_hello_hello_retry_request_rejects_unsolicited_extension</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">missing quic_transport_parameters mapped to handshake_failure instead of missing_extension</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 9001 §8.2: \"MUST close the connection with an error of type 0x016d (equivalent to a fatal TLS missing_extension alert).\" Code used the generic handshake_failure alert (0x128) instead. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664). Fixed: new TlsAlert.missing_extension = 109 (IANA-confirmed), used here.</td>\n      <td>new test <code>test_process_encrypted_extensions_rejects_missing_quic_transport_parameters</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">stateless_reset_token/preferred_address accepted alongside a zero-length server connection ID</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>A server using a zero-length connection ID has no connection ID for either parameter to be anything to do with. Neither decoder can see this cross-field inconsistency alone; only the caller holding peer_initial_scid can. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664). <strong>Honest note:</strong> the exact RFC 9000 §18.2 defining text for these two parameters could not be independently re-fetched this round (the RFC-text tool consistently truncated before that section of a very long document); fixed based on Codex's precise citation plus corroborating adjacent RFC text, not an independently re-verified quote.</td>\n      <td>2 new tests, one per parameter</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">README.md still described the deleted crypto.rsa_pss module as a hard OpenSSL dependency</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>Same class as the PROGRESS.md miss two rounds ago: a doc file not updated when the code it described changed (the module was deleted as dead code in an earlier round). Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664).</td>\n      <td>none (doc-only)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">encode_packet_number silently truncates exhausted packet numbers instead of rejecting them</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>full_pn &ge; 2^62 (RFC 9000 §12.3's own packet-number-space limit) truncates to a 4-byte all-zero field instead of being rejected &mdash; the same wire-integer silent-narrowing class as Angle G3. Codex P2 on #27680 (pullrequestreview-4791164664). Fixed: signature changed from infallible to fallible (no production caller existed yet, a clean change), rejects before the length-selection arithmetic.</td>\n      <td>2 new tests: rejects above the limit, accepts the boundary</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ecdsa key leak, 2nd instance: struct-field-threaded key discarded via <code>_</code> at a caller, not at the constructor</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>tls13_handshake_test.v</code>'s <code>drive_to_wait_encrypted_extensions</code> correctly frees its OWN two local ecdsa keys, but returns a THIRD one bundled inside a <code>FakeServerHandshake.priv_key</code> field. 7 of the file's test functions discarded that struct as <code>_</code>, dropping the only reference to the field's raw <code>EVP_PKEY*</code>; 5 sibling call sites in the SAME file already captured and freed it &mdash; a sibling-parity gap (Phase 0 step 7) within one file's own test helpers, 3 of the 7 leaking functions were themselves NEW in the immediately-prior round. Found by CI (<code>sanitize-address-clang</code>/<code>sanitize-address-gcc</code> LeakSanitizer), not Codex. The existing <code>detect_ecdsa_key_missing_free.sh</code> (built for the FIRST ecdsa-leak miss, rev 21) didn't recognize this file's constructor spelling (<code>generate_key()</code>) at all, so it could not have caught this even if run &mdash; extended to recognize it, but that detector's core \"same variable name, same file\" model is structurally blind to a resource renamed through a struct field and a return; confirmed it STILL reports a false positive on the fixed tree. <strong>Follow-up:</strong> asked directly why this couldn't be mechanized, and it turns out it can &mdash; new <code>detect_struct_wrapped_resource_leak.sh</code> chains struct-field &rarr; returning-function &rarr; call-site lookups and verifies this exact fix cleanly (flags all 7 on the buggy commit, 0 flags on the fixed tree).</td>\n      <td><code>detect_struct_wrapped_resource_leak.sh vlib/net/quic/tls13_handshake_test.v ecdsa\\.\\(PrivateKey\\|PublicKey\\) 66808d3002</code> &mdash; exit 1 (flags all 7 DISCARDED); exit 0 on the fixed tree (genuinely clean, no false positive)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>parse_short_header</code> panics instead of erroring on a negative <code>dcid_len</code></span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>buf.len &lt; 1 + dcid_len</code> doesn't reject a negative <code>dcid_len</code> (both sides go negative together), so the code reaches <code>buf[1..1 + dcid_len]</code> &mdash; a slice with <code>start &gt; end</code>. Reproduced before fixing: <code>parse_short_header([]u8{len: 10, init: 0x40}, -1)</code> crashed with <code>V panic: array.slice: invalid slice index (start&gt;end):1, 0</code>, a genuine crash repro not just a reasoned-through claim. Codex P2 on vlang/v#27680 (pullrequestreview-4806500473). Fixed: explicit <code>dcid_len &lt; 0</code> guard.</td>\n      <td>header_test.v test_short_header_rejects_negative_dcid_len</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">signature_algorithms_cert missing P-384/P-521 ECDSA cert-signing schemes</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Same class as the earlier RSA-PKCS1 fix (rev 25) &mdash; a certificate-signing scheme this client's chain verifier already supports but never advertised. Verified the vendored mbedTLS build actually supports both curves BEFORE adding them: <code>MBEDTLS_ECP_DP_SECP384R1_ENABLED</code>/<code>SECP521R1_ENABLED</code> + <code>MBEDTLS_SHA512_C</code> all confirmed enabled in <code>mbedtls_config.h</code>. <code>signature_algorithms</code> (CertificateVerify) stays P-256-only by design (a separate curve check already enforces this). Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>tls13_client_hello_test.v test_encode_signature_algorithms_cert_extension_wire_format (updated)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">CertificateVerify unoffered-algorithm alert misclassified as decode_error</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>THIRD instance of the error-code-dropped-across-a-caller-boundary pattern (after <code>process_server_hello</code>/<code>process_encrypted_extensions</code>). RFC 8446 &sect;4.4.3 states the algorithm MUST be one offered but, fetched directly, does NOT itself name an alert for the violation &mdash; classified as <code>illegal_parameter</code> via &sect;6.2's general definition, this file's own cipher_suite-not-offered precedent, and majority real-world implementation practice (OpenSSL/BoringSSL/GnuTLS/s2n/wolfSSL), stated as such rather than implying a direct quote. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>tls13_certificate_test.v test_parse_certificate_verify_rejects_unoffered_algorithm (asserts the code); tls13_handshake_test.v test_process_certificate_verify_rejects_unoffered_algorithm</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>encode_packet_number</code>'s 4-byte arm has no upper bound on the packet-number GAP</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>A SECOND, independent bound on a repeat-offender function (rev 25 added the <code>max_packet_number</code> cap on <code>full_pn</code> itself; this caps the GAP relative to <code>largest_acked</code>, reachable even for a small <code>full_pn</code> given a stale/absent <code>largest_acked</code>). Reproduced before fixing: <code>encode_packet_number(max_packet_number, Some(0))</code> produced 4 bytes that <code>decode_packet_number</code> reconstructed as 2^32-1, not the 2^62-1 actually sent. Re-derived the function's full contract from scratch per the repeat-offender rule and confirmed the two bounds are logically independent. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>packet_number_test.v test_packet_number_encode_rejects_gap_too_large_for_four_bytes, test_packet_number_encode_accepts_boundary_four_byte_gap</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">HelloRetryRequest treated <code>key_share</code> as mandatory</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 8446 &sect;4.1.4, fetched directly: \"The server's extensions MUST contain 'supported_versions'\" &mdash; key_share not named as mandatory, and a HelloRetryRequest MAY request only a cookie round-trip when the client's already-offered share is acceptable. Rejected a valid cookie-only HRR before it could ever be represented. Fixed: <code>ParsedHelloRetryRequest.selected_group</code> is now <code>?u16</code>; the one downstream consumer validates the group only when present. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>tls13_server_hello_test.v test_parse_server_hello_hello_retry_request_cookie_only</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>server_name</code> in EncryptedExtensions not checked for empty <code>extension_data</code></span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 6066 &sect;3, fetched directly and confirmed verbatim: \"the server SHALL include an extension of type 'server_name' ... The 'extension_data' field of this extension SHALL be empty.\" A non-empty payload passed through silently. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>tls13_server_hello_test.v test_parse_encrypted_extensions_accepts_empty_server_name, test_parse_encrypted_extensions_rejects_nonempty_server_name</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>preferred_address</code> IPv4/IPv6 address+port pairs not checked for consistency</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 9000 &sect;18.2 defines all-zero address+port TOGETHER as \"not provided\"; the RFC text does not appear to explicitly define a MISMATCHED pair &mdash; marked 🔸 defensive, like this file's own duplicate-parameter-ID precedent, not a confirmed literal MUST. Fixed symmetrically on both <code>encode_preferred_address</code> and <code>decode_preferred_address</code> (an asymmetric one-sided fix is exactly this file's own previously-corrected bug shape). Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>transport_parameters_test.v test_encode_preferred_address_rejects_mismatched_address_port, test_encode_preferred_address_rejects_mismatched_ipv6_address_port, test_decode_preferred_address_rejects_mismatched_address_port</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ServerNameList/client_shares length bounds ignore their own wrapping overhead &mdash; PARTIALLY confirmed</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>The bound was genuinely wrong (didn't account for the ServerNameList/client_shares own length-prefix overhead), but the claimed CONSEQUENCE &mdash; \"silently succeeds, producing a malformed ClientHello\" &mdash; was traced through the arithmetic and empirically refuted: <code>encode_extension</code>'s own generic <code>data.len &gt; 0xffff</code> guard already rejects every value the loose bound let through (confirmed with a direct test against the unfixed code, which returned an error, not a success, for the exact value the finding named). Fixed anyway at the correct layer, for a correctly-attributed error message; found the identical sibling gap in <code>encode_key_share_extension</code> proactively. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4806500473). <strong>Class: a reviewer's citation being wrong does not automatically make their claimed consequence true &mdash; trace the actual downstream guards before accepting \"no error was returned\" as given.</strong></td>\n      <td>tls13_client_hello_test.v test_encode_server_name_extension_rejects_hostname_near_65535_bound, test_encode_server_name_extension_accepts_boundary_hostname_length</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>PROGRESS.md</code> still marked the removed <code>rsa_pss</code> module as complete</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>Same class as the README.md miss from two rounds ago &mdash; a doc file not updated when the code it described changed, recurring a second time in a DIFFERENT doc file for the SAME underlying removal. Codex P3 (pullrequestreview-4806500473).</td>\n      <td>none (doc-only)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>encode_packet_number</code>'s \"no ack yet\" branch never checked <code>full_pn</code> fits in the 4-byte encoding it always chooses</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>num_unacked</code> in this branch is a fixed sentinel (<code>0x7FFF_FFFF</code>), never derived from <code>full_pn</code>, so the round-6 gap check (<code>(num_unacked+1)*2 &gt; 2^32</code>) is permanently false here regardless of <code>full_pn</code>'s actual size. Reproduced: <code>encode_packet_number(0x1_0000_0000, none)</code> returned 4 zero bytes; decode reconstructed 0, not 2^32. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4822597219). Third independent bound found on this repeat-offender function across 3 rounds.</td>\n      <td>packet_number_test.v test_packet_number_encode_rejects_oversized_first_packet_in_space, test_packet_number_encode_accepts_boundary_first_packet_in_space</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>parse_certificate</code> never validated CertificateEntry extension legality</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 8446 &sect;4.2's table permits only <code>status_request</code>/<code>signed_certificate_timestamp</code> in a CertificateEntry; <code>supported_versions</code> (Codex's example) is CH/SH/HRR-only. This client offers NEITHER of the two CT-legal extensions, so the correct fix rejects ANY extension present, broader than the literal finding. Reproduced: a synthetic Certificate message with a <code>supported_versions</code> CertificateEntry extension parsed with no error. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4822597219).</td>\n      <td>tls13_certificate_test.v test_parse_certificate_rejects_entry_with_illegal_extension</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">Self-caught: fixing the row above discarded its own new error code across a caller boundary</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td><code>process_certificate_or_request</code> unconditionally remapped ANY <code>parse_certificate</code> failure to a generic <code>decode_error</code>, discarding the new <code>unsupported_extension</code> classification above &mdash; the exact <code>err.code() != 0</code> preservation pattern already used by 3 OTHER call sites in the SAME file, just never applied to this fourth one. Reproduced: driving a real handshake to a tampered Certificate and checking <code>err.code()</code> returned decode_error's code, not the expected one. <strong>Not a Codex finding &mdash; caught by this round's own /vreview pass before push.</strong></td>\n      <td>tls13_handshake_test.v test_process_certificate_or_request_preserves_illegal_extension_code</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\"><code>supported_groups</code> extension's inner NamedGroupList framing never validated</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>RFC 8446 &sect;4.2.7 / RFC 7919's NamedGroupList is a 2-byte length prefix + that many bytes of 2-byte NamedGroup entries; only the OUTER extension TLV framing was ever checked. Reproduced: extension_data <code>[0x00, 0x02, 0xAA]</code> (inner length claims 2 bytes, 1 present) parsed with no error. This client never reads this field's value (key exchange goes through <code>key_share</code>). Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4822597219).</td>\n      <td>tls13_server_hello_test.v test_parse_encrypted_extensions_accepts_wellformed_supported_groups, test_parse_encrypted_extensions_rejects_malformed_supported_groups</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">ALPN protocol names required to be unique &mdash; REFUTED as an RFC requirement, implemented anyway on maintainer request</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Fetched RFC 7301 &sect;3.1 directly: it forbids empty/truncated strings but states no uniqueness requirement anywhere. <code>protocols</code> is caller-supplied (a duplicate is reachable) but creates no wire-format or protocol ambiguity &mdash; kept off the baseline in round 7, no code change at the time. Codex P2 (pullrequestreview-4822597219). A maintainer (JalonSolov) then followed up on the thread agreeing with the RFC read but requesting the check anyway as hygiene (wasted wire bytes, no negotiation benefit, discussion_r3690498809) &mdash; landed in vlang/v#28001: <code>encode_alpn_extension</code> now tracks seen names via a <code>map[string]bool</code> and rejects a repeat. <strong>Lesson: a REFUTED citation doesn't end the conversation &mdash; a maintainer can still want the behavior for reasons the RFC doesn't cover.</strong></td>\n      <td>tls13_client_hello_test.v test_build_client_hello_rejects_duplicate_alpn_protocols, test_build_client_hello_accepts_distinct_alpn_protocols</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">WebFetch truncates before reaching late RFC sections</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Methodology gap, not a code bug. Verifying two Codex findings on vlang/v#27880 (Retry dcid/scid field semantics, RFC 9000 &sect;17.2.5.1; CRYPTO frame's 2^62-1 offset+length bound, &sect;19.6) against the primary text repeatedly failed &mdash; <code>WebFetch</code> on <code>rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html</code>/<code>.txt</code> summarized only the first few thousand lines and reported the target section \"not included in the provided excerpt\" even though &sect;17/&sect;19 are real, later sections of the SAME ~8500-line document; the single-shot summarization has a content budget the RFC exceeds. <strong>Fix: cache the plain-text RFC locally</strong> (<code>curl -sL https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc&lt;N&gt;.txt -o rfc&lt;N&gt;.txt</code>) to <code>.claude/skills/code-review/rfc-texts/</code> (confirmed gitignored), then grep/read the exact section directly instead of re-fetching through the summarizer. Also checked RFC errata (<code>errata.rfc-editor.org/search/?rfc_number=&lt;N&gt;</code> via WebFetch &mdash; the <code>errata_search.php?format=json</code> endpoint is JS-rendered and returns only a search-form shell to curl) before trusting the cached text as final; confirmed none of the sections actually used (&sect;6.2, &sect;12.2, &sect;14.1, &sect;17.2.5, &sect;19.3, &sect;19.6) have outstanding errata. RFC 9000/9001/9002/8446/7301 now cached locally with a README documenting the errata-check status and refresh command &mdash; reusable for every future QUIC/TLS/HTTP3 review.</td>\n      <td>n/a (new technique, not a detector) &mdash; <code>.claude/skills/code-review/rfc-texts/README.md</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">unshared getter return: internal state returned by reference, not copy</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 4 on vlang/v#27880 (three rounds after the file was first written): <code>CryptoStreamReassembler.data()</code> (<code>crypto_stream.v</code>) returned <code>r.received</code> directly, no <code>.clone()</code> &mdash; V's plain array assignment shares backing storage, so <code>mut d := r.data(); d[0] = x</code> corrupted the reassembler's own CRYPTO transcript, despite its own doc comment saying \"this is a growing snapshot, not a destructive drain\". <code>data()</code> was never touched by any of the three prior review rounds on this same file &mdash; the repeat-offender full-contract-re-derivation rule was being applied only to functions with a fresh diff each round, not to every function in a file that already qualified as a repeat offender. Built and Phase-R verified a mechanical detector (exit 1 on <code>72a6269e1e</code>, exit 0 on the <code>.clone()</code> fix), then pointed it at the rest of the module: immediately found two more, still-open instances one file away (<code>Tls13ClientHandshake.application_secrets()</code>/<code>handshake_secrets()</code> in <code>tls13_handshake.v</code>, each returning a struct with <code>[]u8</code> secret fields) &mdash; proof the detector generalizes past the one instance it was written for, not just re-detecting the original. Flagged those two via a separate task rather than fixing inline (different phase/branch, zero current callers at the time); fixed in a follow-up session on the <code>http3-quic-handshake-completion</code> branch with the same <code>.clone()</code>-per-field pattern plus a regression test proving independence via <code>unsafe</code> raw-pointer writes (the only reachable exploit path, since V's checker already blocks both the naive direct-mutation and a plain <code>mut</code>-alias of the <code>pub:</code> fields).</td>\n      <td><code>detect_unshared_getter_return.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">stale/backwards &#8987; DEFERRED rows in a standing conformance matrix</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 6 on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4839675790): <code>split_coalesced_datagram</code> never checked the overall datagram size, so an Initial packet in a UDP datagram under RFC 9000 &sect;14.1's 1200-byte floor was still returned &mdash; no compliant peer, client or server, can legitimately produce one this small (both sides pad Initial-carrying datagrams to that floor). Fixed by excluding just the undersized packet (mirroring the file's existing DCID-mismatch skip-and-continue pattern), not erroring the whole call. <strong>The self-found part, larger than the original finding:</strong> <code>quic_conformance_matrix.md</code> already had a row for this requirement, written &#8987; DEFERRED before Phase 4 landed on this branch &mdash; conflating BOTH halves of &sect;14.1 (send-side padding, implemented; receive-side discard, not) under one send-side-sounding title. Sweeping every other &#8987; row for the same staleness pattern found 3 more: two reserved-bits rows (long/short header) fully resolved by <code>header_protection.v</code> once Phase 3 landed, and &mdash; worse &mdash; a Version-Negotiation-discard row whose own WRITTEN DESCRIPTION had RFC 9000 &sect;6.2 backwards (said \"discard when unsupported, error when v1 present,\" the exact inverse of the real rule and of the correct, already-fixed code from finding F1). A wrong row is more dangerous than a missing one: a future reviewer trusting it could \"fix\" correct code to match it. <strong>Root cause:</strong> the matrix's own \"How to use\" section already stated the re-audit rule, but it was never actually executed across five full Codex rounds on this exact code &mdash; a written rule with no checkable trigger doesn't get followed. Fix (process): added an explicit step&nbsp;0 &mdash; grep &#8987; and cross-check each cited file against <code>git log &lt;base&gt;..HEAD -- &lt;file&gt;</code> before using the matrix to argue \"already covered,\" run at the start of every Codex-round triage.</td>\n      <td>none (the matrix IS the detector) &mdash; <code>grep -n \"&#8987;\" quic_conformance_matrix.md</code> then <code>git log --oneline &lt;base&gt;..HEAD -- &lt;cited-file&gt;</code> per hit</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">deferred-state doc comment understated its own RFC requirement</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 7 on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4840201604): <code>verify_retry_integrity_tag</code>'s own doc comment said RFC 9000 &sect;17.2.5.2 requires discarding only \"a second Retry\" &mdash; the actual second sentence is broader (\"after processing an Initial OR Retry, discard any subsequent Retry\"), so a correctly-tagged Retry arriving after the client already processed the server's Initial still passed both <code>parse_retry_packet</code> and this verifier, since neither received that connection state. Fixed by adding <code>already_processed_other_packet bool</code>, checked before tag verification &mdash; an exact mirror of <code>version_negotiation.v</code>'s existing parameter of the same name for the analogous &sect;6.2 VN-discard requirement (same PR, earlier round), rather than re-deferring with just a corrected comment. <strong>Two files in the SAME PR had handled structurally identical \"discard once other connection state exists\" requirements differently</strong> (VN: implemented; Retry: deferred-and-under-documented) until this fix brought them to parity. <code>parse_retry_packet</code>'s doc comment now also states the calling-order contract this establishes (verify first, only parse what verify accepts). Regression test proves the discard is attributable to the flag, not an incidental tag mismatch: the same packet verifies true with the flag false, false with it true.</td>\n      <td>none (mirrors an existing sibling pattern) &mdash; retry_test.v test_verify_retry_integrity_tag_discards_when_already_processed_other_packet</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">wire-integer overflow: offset+length addition wraps for an offset near u64::MAX</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 7 on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4840201604): <code>CryptoStreamReassembler.add</code>'s <code>end := offset + u64(data.len)</code> wraps to a small value when <code>offset</code> is near <code>u64::MAX</code>, silently passing the <code>end &gt; max_crypto_stream_buffered_bytes</code> guard computed from the wrapped result &mdash; exactly Angle G3's named pattern (\"the guard sees the wrapped result and incorrectly accepts it\"), missed because the function's only current production caller already bounds <code>offset+length &le; 2^62-1</code> upstream, making the check look unnecessary from a diff-only read. Fixed by rejecting on <code>offset</code> alone before computing the sum, which also makes the addition provably overflow-free afterward. Phase-R'd: <code>r.add(max_u64, [1]u8)</code> succeeded pre-fix, rejects post-fix. <strong>Lesson: an upstream caller already bounding a value does not exempt a <code>pub fn</code>'s OWN stated contract from defending itself independently</strong> &mdash; this function's own doc comment for <code>max_crypto_stream_buffered_bytes</code> explicitly frames the bound as the standalone defense against \"a malicious or buggy peer,\" not as something contingent on what today's one caller happens to already check.</td>\n      <td>crypto_stream_test.v test_crypto_stream_reassembler_rejects_offset_near_u64_max</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">a prior round's OWN fix regressed a legitimate case, because a requirement's literal subject was read out of its role-scope</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">E</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 8 on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4843018164): round 6 added an unconditional discard of any Initial packet in a datagram under RFC 9000 &sect;14.1's 1200-byte floor, citing \"A server MUST discard an Initial packet...\" &mdash; but that MUST binds a SERVER receiving an undersized CLIENT Initial (anti-amplification), and a server's OWN Initial is only required to be padded when it's ACK-ELICITING (Table 3 marks ACK/PADDING/CONNECTION_CLOSE \"N\", not ack-eliciting) &mdash; so a legitimate server response containing only e.g. an ACK or an early CONNECTION_CLOSE can be smaller than 1200 bytes, and round 6's fix discarded it. <strong>The same \"citation and consequence are two separate things\" discipline already in <code>/codex-respond</code> Phase 2, just missed on my OWN prior fix rather than on a reviewer's finding</strong> &mdash; the 1200-byte NUMBER and the general shape of the requirement were verified against the primary RFC text, but never WHO the \"MUST discard\" bound (server, not any receiver) or under what CONDITION the sender-side pairing requirement applied (ack-eliciting only). Reverted entirely, not narrowed: this splitter has no visibility into frame contents (still AEAD-protected) to tell the legitimate case apart from a non-compliant one, and this codebase is client-only (Phase 13/server explicitly deferred) so the server-side half has no role to attach to yet. Reverted the 3 test-padding additions and the dedicated regression test from round 6 back to original form; corrected the <code>quic_conformance_matrix.md</code> row this same finding had created (previously erroneously &#10004;) to N/A/out-of-scope, with the two-round history kept as a cautionary example in the row itself.</td>\n      <td>none &mdash; required checking the requirement's applicable ROLE against what this codebase implements, a judgment call not a syntactic pattern</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">function's own doc comment stated a bool contract; one branch still errored, contradicting it</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>Codex round 8 on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4843018164), P1: round 7 rewrote <code>verify_retry_integrity_tag</code>'s doc comment to say \"FALSE means discard it... TRUE means...\" &mdash; a strict bool contract &mdash; while adding a NEW bool-returning early-return, but left the PRE-EXISTING too-short-packet branch as <code>return error(...)</code>, unnoticed, in the SAME edit that added the doc comment describing the contract that branch violates. A caller propagating <code>!</code> on that error would treat an unverifiable (not merely invalid-tag) Retry as connection-fatal, when RFC 9000 &sect;17.2.5.2 requires discarding it silently, identically to a tag mismatch. Fixed: the too-short branch now returns <code>false</code>. <strong>Root cause, precisely named:</strong> the \"read your own doc comment as a checklist against your own code\" discipline (established after round 3's citation-shaped-fix miss on this SAME PR) was applied to the NEW code being written that round, but not swept across the REST of the function's pre-existing body in the same edit &mdash; the repeat-offender full-contract-re-derivation rule should have caught this, since this function was touched again in the SAME round it was supposed to protect against exactly this. Updated the one existing test asserting the old error behavior to assert <code>false</code>.</td>\n      <td>none (an internal self-consistency check the \"read your own doc comment as a checklist\" discipline should have caught, but didn't, on the SAME round that wrote the doc comment)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">Skill tool served a stale, pre-edit cached copy of SKILL.md mid-session</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">Process</span></td>\n      <td>The user directly edited <code>.claude/skills/vreview/SKILL.md</code> on disk mid-session (a 10-revision process-improvement pass: Review Modes, a new Angle H, required-artifacts blocks, a 6-column sibling parity table, 3 concurrency matrices), synced into this HTML reference up through rev 41. Invoking the <code>Skill</code> tool for <code>/vreview</code> AFTER those edits still returned the OLD content verbatim (no Angle H, no Review Modes, still \"A-G\") &mdash; confirmed by reading the actual file on disk directly (found Angle H at line 1502; the Skill tool's returned text had no such section). Surfaced by the user's own question (\"Why no /vreview\") after an ad-hoc inline equivalent was run instead of actually invoking the skill. <strong>Fix used this session:</strong> read SKILL.md directly with the file-read tool instead of trusting the Skill tool's returned content, then manually applied the CURRENT process (Review Mode selection, the expanded checklist, Angle H's absence-search, the full Artifacts block) by hand. <strong>Open question, not yet answered:</strong> whether every <code>/vreview</code> invocation earlier in this session, after the edits landed, was silently reviewing against the stale pre-edit process.</td>\n      <td>none found &mdash; no way to force-refresh the Skill tool's cache from within a session; read the file directly as a workaround</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">new function's doc comment omitted a compositional detail every real caller already relied on</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">I</span></td>\n      <td><code>pad_initial_payload(payload, header_len, aead_tag_len)</code>'s doc comment described <code>header_len</code> only as \"bytes of already-built header,\" never stating it must include the packet-number field length &mdash; yet its one real caller (<code>initial_exchange_test.v</code>) already passed <code>encode_long_header(...).len + pn_length</code>. The function's own body was unaffected (it only sums <code>header_len + payload.len + aead_tag_len</code> against the 1200-byte floor) &mdash; a future caller reading only the doc could pass the bare header length and silently under-pad below RFC 9000 &sect;14.1's floor by 1-4 bytes, with no test catching it since every EXISTING caller already (correctly, undocumented-ly) included the PN bytes. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4875246955). <strong>Root cause: this function was itself written inside a round-6 FIX commit responding to a different Codex finding &mdash; fix-commit verification checks \"does this satisfy the reported finding,\" not \"does this new function's doc fully specify its own contract.\" No existing angle asked whether a doc comment's stated parameter semantics matched real call-site expressions &mdash; this became Angle I.</strong> Fixed in commit <code>e25ebddc27</code>.</td>\n      <td>none &mdash; requires diffing a doc comment's stated parameter semantics against real call-site expressions by hand; no automatable pattern</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">existing test predated a later doc-comment contract tightening, never swept for compliance</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">I</span></td>\n      <td><code>test_retry_packet_round_trip_verifies</code> called <code>parse_retry_packet</code> before <code>verify_retry_integrity_tag</code>, contradicting <code>parse_retry_packet</code>'s own doc comment (\"Callers MUST call verify_retry_integrity_tag first\"). The test was written on day one of Phase 4, before that MUST-verify-first sentence existed &mdash; the sentence was added later, in round 7, as a doc-only clarification. Round 8 re-derived <code>verify_retry_integrity_tag</code>'s full contract from scratch per the repeat-offender rule and checked it against the function's OWN body (correctly catching that round's real P1) &mdash; but never widened to grep the function's EXISTING callers for compliance. The test passed either way (fixture data, not untrusted input), so no functional-behavior check would ever surface this. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27880 (pullrequestreview-4875246955). <strong>Root cause: \"impact analysis before narrowing behavior\" only triggers on BEHAVIORAL changes &mdash; a doc-only contract clarification that changes nothing at runtime slips past it entirely, since there's no test failure to catch it.</strong> Fixed in commit <code>e25ebddc27</code>.</td>\n      <td>none &mdash; requires re-checking existing callers/tests against a contract tightened in a LATER commit, not just the touched function's own body</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">repeat-offender fix closed the NAMED gap but the SAME derivation re-derived a field the spec says must NOT change</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>next_key_resolution</code>/<code>commit_new_update</code> (<code>key_update.v</code>) both called <code>derive_packet_protection_keys(new_secret)</code>, deriving <code>key</code>/<code>iv</code>/<code>hp</code> all fresh &mdash; but RFC 9001 &sect;6 rotates ONLY <code>key</code>/<code>iv</code> on a 1-RTT key update; <code>hp</code> is derived once per encryption level and never updates within it. Both call sites had already been touched once on this branch (an earlier Phase-R fix for a DIFFERENT bug in the same function family) &mdash; the repeat-offender full-contract-re-derivation rule fired for <code>note_successful_decrypt</code> itself but never extended to its two sibling helpers, which share the identical derive-then-store shape and the identical missed per-field constraint. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27881 (pullrequestreview-4885595852). <strong>Root cause: a prior Phase-R fix scoped to \"re-derive the classification correctly\" verified against ONE reported symptom; never asked whether OTHER fields the same derivation touches have a DIFFERENT rotation rule.</strong> Fixed via new <code>derive_updated_packet_protection_keys(next_secret, current_hp)</code> helper preserving <code>hp</code>. Fixed in commit <code>e2e09bcb59</code>.</td>\n      <td>none yet &mdash; candidate future Angle A bullet: \"does this rotation correctly hold ONE field constant while others advance\"</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">fix's own doc comment claimed correctness \"regardless of order\" without checking a single-bit field's PERIOD against a repeated event</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>note_successful_decrypt</code>'s doc comment (written in a PRIOR Phase-R fix, same PR) claimed \"correct regardless of how many resolutions were computed before it, or in what order they are committed\" &mdash; verified only against a two-resolution, ONE-update scenario. After a SECOND key update, the phase bit (period-2) cycles back to its generation-0 value, so a genuinely old generation-0 packet's phase bit coincidentally matches the new generation-2 current phase, corrupting <code>min_pn_in_current_phase</code> &mdash; the exact hazard the doc comment claimed was solved, recurring because the classification key (a 1-bit phase) carries strictly less information than what it must distinguish (an unbounded generation count). GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27881 (pullrequestreview-4885595852). <strong>Root cause: the prior fix's regression test only exercised ONE update &mdash; scoped to the reported symptom, not to the INVARIANT the fix's own doc comment claimed (\"regardless of order\" implicitly promises correctness across arbitrarily many updates).</strong> Fixed via an absolute <code>current_generation</code>/<code>KeyResolution.generation</code> counter compared directly instead of re-derived from the lossy 1-bit phase. Fixed in commit <code>e2e09bcb59</code>.</td>\n      <td>none yet &mdash; candidate future check: for any boolean/single-bit classification of a REPEATABLE event, does period-N aliasing after N repeats defeat it</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">missing pub fn doc comments (3&times; in one round) &mdash; report-missing-fn-doc CI check would have caught them</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>new_packet_number_spaces</code>, <code>KeyUpdateState.current_phase_bit</code>, <code>new_handshake_completion_state</code> were all pub fns with no doc comment. AGENTS.md's style rule only specifies the FORMAT once a comment exists (\"Doc comments start with function name\") &mdash; it was never operationalized as \"every pub fn, scan for absence.\" GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27881 (pullrequestreview-4885595852). Added an explicit Angle A bullet requiring this as a pure grep, not a judgment call. Fixed in commit <code>e2e09bcb59</code>.</td>\n      <td>new Angle A bullet (mandatory doc-comment-presence check, added rev 45)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">fix's own doc comment claimed correctness \"regardless of order\" without checking a single-bit field's PERIOD against repeated events</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>key_update.v</code> <code>note_successful_decrypt</code>'s doc comment (written in a PRIOR Phase-R fix, same PR) claimed \"correct regardless of how many resolutions were computed before it, or in what order they are committed\" -- verified only against a two-resolution, ONE-update scenario. After a SECOND key update, the phase bit (period-2) cycles back to its generation-0 value, so a genuinely old generation-0 packet's phase bit coincidentally matches the new generation-2 current phase, corrupting <code>min_pn_in_current_phase</code> -- the exact hazard the doc comment claimed was solved, recurring because the classification key (a 1-bit phase) carries strictly less information than what it must distinguish (an unbounded generation count). GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27881 (pullrequestreview-4885595852). <strong>Root cause: the prior fix's regression test only exercised ONE update -- scoped to the reported symptom, not to the INVARIANT the fix's own doc comment claimed.</strong> Fixed via an absolute <code>current_generation</code>/<code>KeyResolution.generation</code> counter compared directly instead of re-derived from the lossy 1-bit phase. Fixed in commit <code>e2e09bcb59</code>.</td>\n      <td>none yet -- candidate future check: for any boolean/single-bit classification of a REPEATABLE event, does period-N aliasing after N repeats defeat it</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">accumulating buffer's own doc comment claimed a cap \"rarely binds in practice,\" but the buffer had no drain path at all, making the cap a hard total-size ceiling</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>stream_reassembly.v</code> <code>StreamReassembler.received</code>/<code>add</code>: <code>max_stream_buffered_bytes</code>'s doc comment claimed real flow control would make the 1 MiB cap \"rarely matter in practice,\" but <code>received</code> only ever appended (<code>&lt;&lt;</code>), never shrank -- no <code>discard</code>/<code>consume</code>/drain existed. This made 1 MiB the EFFECTIVE TOTAL SIZE CEILING for any stream, even entirely in-order data, not a rarely-hit backstop. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27882 (pullrequestreview-4888843234). <strong>Root cause: read this file in full during my own <code>/vreview</code> pass and read the doc comment's claim, but never asked \"does this field ever get smaller\" -- a doc comment's stated safety rationale was taken at face value instead of traced against the field's actual lifecycle.</strong> Fixed by adding <code>base_offset</code> + a <code>discard(new_base)</code> method so a future caller (Phase 9's application-read path) can free consumed bytes, sliding the cap to bound buffered-but-unconsumed data instead of total stream size. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_stream_reassembler_supports_streams_larger_than_the_buffering_limit</code> sends &gt;1 MiB total via repeated add+discard; fails to reach that total on pre-fix code, passes after.</td>\n      <td>new Angle D bullet (\"accumulating-buffer capacity claims require a drain path\")</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">self-inflicted: copied a parse-side overflow-safe check's SHAPE onto the encode side without re-verifying its precondition (bounded operands) held there</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>frame.v</code> <code>encode_stream_frame</code>: in an EARLIER round of this same review, added <code>if offset + u64(data.len) &gt; max_varint</code>, copied from <code>parse_crypto_frame</code>'s own (correctly overflow-safe) identical-looking check -- but that check's safety argument depends on both operands coming from <code>decode_varint</code>, inherently bounded to max_varint; <code>encode_stream_frame</code>'s <code>offset</code> is a caller-supplied parameter with NO such bound, so the sum itself could overflow u64 for an offset near <code>u64::MAX</code>, wrapping to a small value and silently passing the check it was meant to enforce. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27882 (pullrequestreview-4888843234). <strong>Root cause: copied a validated pattern's TEXT without re-deriving whether its PRECONDITION held at the new (encode, not parse) call site.</strong> Fixed with an overflow-safe form: bound <code>offset</code> alone first, then a subtraction-based comparison that can never itself overflow. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_encode_stream_frame_rejects_offset_near_u64_max_without_overflow</code> passes <code>offset = max_u64 - 3, data.len = 5</code>; silently succeeds (should reject) on pre-fix code, rejects after.</td>\n      <td>Angle G3 extended (encode-side/caller-supplied values are NOT automatically safe just because they mirror a parse-side check that is)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">fixed a bound-check gap on one struct's field but never grepped for the SAME field name on a sibling struct in the same file</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>frame.v</code> <code>StreamsBlockedFrame.maximum_streams</code> (vs. <code>MaxStreamsFrame.maximum_streams</code>, fixed one round earlier): fixed <code>MaxStreamsFrame</code>'s missing RFC 9000 &sect;4.6 2^60 cap in response to my own earlier <code>/vreview</code> finding, but never grepped <code>maximum_streams</code> across the rest of <code>frame.v</code> -- <code>StreamsBlockedFrame</code> carries the identical semantic value with the identical requirement stated in ITS OWN RFC section (&sect;19.14), a few dozen lines away in the same file. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27882 (pullrequestreview-4888843234). <strong>Root cause: anchored the sibling search on \"which OTHER function covers the same RFC section I just cited\" rather than \"which OTHER struct/field shares this literal name\" -- the field-name grep is cheaper and file-scope, independent of which RFC section documents each frame type's own copy of the requirement.</strong> Fixed by adding the identical bound to both <code>parse_streams_blocked_frame</code> and <code>encode_streams_blocked_frame</code>. <strong>Repro:</strong> STREAMS_BLOCKED boundary test passes <code>max_initial_max_streams + 1</code>; silently accepted on pre-fix code, rejected after.</td>\n      <td>new Phase 0 step 7 addendum (grep the field/semantic name, not just the RFC section, after any bound fix)</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">reordered-arrival clobbered a terminal state (RFC 9000 &sect;3.2 Reset Recvd) via an unrelated code path that never checked for it, 3x (same finding, 3 call sites)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>stream.v</code> <code>StreamRecvHalf.note_size_known</code>/<code>note_data</code> (vs. <code>mark_reset_recvd</code>): <code>mark_reset_recvd</code> set <code>.reset_recvd</code>, but <code>note_size_known</code>/<code>note_data</code> -- driven by ordinary STREAM-frame arrival, with no awareness that RESET_STREAM might have already been processed -- unconditionally promoted state to <code>.data_recvd</code> once enough bytes had accumulated, with no check for the CURRENT state already being terminal. A STREAM frame in flight before a RESET_STREAM raced ahead of it (normal packet reordering) would silently un-reset the stream's classification. GitHub Copilot review on vlang/v#27882 (pullrequestreview-4888843234). <strong>Root cause: no existing angle asked \"for a terminal state value, does every OTHER writer of this field respect it\" -- the closest existing coverage (flag-contract/sibling-boolean-priority bullets) covers MULTIPLE flags interacting, not ONE terminal enum value against every other writer of the same field.</strong> Fixed by making both functions no-op once <code>state</code> is <code>.reset_recvd</code>/<code>.reset_read</code>. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_recv_half_reset_recvd_is_not_clobbered_by_a_reordered_data_frame</code>/<code>..._size_known_frame</code> call <code>mark_reset_recvd</code> then <code>note_data</code>/<code>note_size_known</code>; state silently flips to <code>.data_recvd</code> on pre-fix code, stays <code>.reset_recvd</code> after.</td>\n      <td>new Angle D bullet (\"terminal state protection\")</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">a struct's own doc comment promised a downstream validation call that actually lived in a DIFFERENT function/file, and that call was never wired in</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>frame.v</code> <code>ResetStreamFrame</code>'s own doc comment claimed its <code>final_size</code> field is \"reconciled against any data already received via <code>StreamReassembler.note_final_size</code> (FINAL_SIZE_ERROR on mismatch)\" -- but <code>stream.v</code> <code>mark_reset_recvd</code>, the actual function that processes a RESET_STREAM event, never called <code>note_final_size</code> at all, nor even stored <code>final_size</code> on the recv half. A reset claiming a final size smaller than data already received, or disagreeing with an already-established final size, was silently accepted. Maintainer-run \"Local AI Review\" on vlang/v#27882 (2026-08-11, top-level PR comment). <strong>Root cause: the doc-comment-vs-code self-consistency check from [[feedback_vreview_repeat_offender_gate]] (\"read your own fix's doc comment literally against its own code\") was applied only WITHIN a function/file, never CROSS-FILE -- a struct's doc comment in one file can make a claim about behavior that's supposed to live in an entirely different function, and nothing prompted grepping for whether that promised call site actually exists.</strong> Fixed by threading <code>final_size</code> through <code>mark_reset_recvd</code> (now returns <code>!</code>) into <code>reassembler.note_final_size</code>, verified against RFC 9000 &sect;3.2's actual state diagram (fetched, not assumed) that a reset from <code>data_recvd</code> is legitimate rather than something to reject outright. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_recv_half_reset_recvd_rejects_final_size_smaller_than_already_received</code>/<code>..._conflicting_with_known_final_size</code>; Phase-R'd by temporarily removing just the <code>note_final_size</code> call and confirming both tests fail, then restoring it.</td>\n      <td>candidate future check: grep other structs'/functions' doc comments for a reference to THIS function by name (\"reconciled via X\", \"validated by Y\"), and verify X/Y is actually called from the entry point the comment is attached to -- a cross-file extension of the existing doc-comment-vs-code self-consistency check</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">two sibling-parity misses in the same external-review round: a guarded-transition pattern one sibling already had wasn't applied to its terminal-state sibling; an input-validation pattern one sibling already had wasn't applied to its read-side sibling</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>stream.v</code> <code>mark_reset_sent</code> (vs. sibling <code>mark_fin_sent</code>, same struct, a few lines above): <code>mark_fin_sent</code> already guards its transition (<code>if h.state == .ready || h.state == .send</code>), but <code>mark_reset_sent</code> unconditionally set state to <code>.reset_sent</code> even from the terminal states <code>.data_recvd</code>/<code>.reset_recvd</code> -- RFC 9000 &sect;3.1's Sending Stream State Machine (fetched and verified) draws \"Send RESET_STREAM\" only from Ready/Send/Data Sent, and its prose states a sender MUST NOT send RESET_STREAM from a terminal state. Separately, <code>flow_control.v</code> <code>ReceiveWindow.note_read</code> (vs. sibling <code>note_received</code>, same struct): <code>note_received</code> already validates its input against <code>w.advertised</code> (returns a FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR on violation), but <code>note_read</code> had no equivalent check against <code>w.received</code>, letting <code>should_advertise_more()</code> grant credit for bytes never actually received. Maintainer-run \"Local AI Review\" on vlang/v#27882 (2026-08-11, top-level PR comment). <strong>Root cause: both are exactly the class of gap Phase 0 step 7's sibling-parity search already targets -- an EXISTING angle that simply wasn't applied thoroughly enough during this branch's own prior <code>/vreview</code> passes on these same two files.</strong> Fixed: <code>mark_reset_sent</code> now no-ops from either terminal state; <code>note_read</code> now caps its input to <code>w.received</code>. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_send_half_reset_sent_does_not_regress_from_data_recvd</code>/<code>..._from_reset_recvd</code>, <code>test_receive_window_note_read_is_capped_to_received</code> -- all 3 Phase-R'd against unfixed code before the fix landed.</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- reinforces existing Phase 0 step 7 (sibling search); a reminder that having the angle documented doesn't guarantee it's exhaustively applied to every function pair in a file, only that it's checked</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">openssl gate missed by two consecutive phases (7, then 8)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">Process</span></td>\n      <td>Phase 7 (PR #27884) and Phase 8 (PR #27885) each added new <code>vlib/net/quic/*_test.v</code> files without the <code>// vtest build: present_openssl?</code> gate every other quic test file carries. Since <code>module quic</code> compiles as ONE translation unit, any ungated file hard-fails <code>docker-ubuntu-musl</code> CI (<code>fatal error: sys/cdefs.h: No such file or directory</code>) via <code>tls13_handshake.v</code>&rsquo;s transitive <code>crypto.ecdsa</code> OpenSSL dependency &mdash; caught both times by CI, not review, since nothing enforced the convention mechanically before a diff was pushed. <strong>Fix:</strong> gated the 5 newly-added Phase 8 files (<code>connection_close_test.v</code>, <code>ecn_test.v</code>, <code>idle_timeout_test.v</code>, <code>pmtu_test.v</code>, <code>stateless_reset_test.v</code>), same pattern as PR #27884&rsquo;s own fix (commit <code>39c3f2f414</code>), verified both directions (SKIP with <code>PKG_CONFIG_PATH</code> pointed nowhere; pass with real OpenSSL), and wrote a detector so a THIRD phase cannot repeat this silently.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_quic_test_missing_openssl_gate.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">scheduler aggregator missing new deadline field</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td><code>QuicConn.closing_deadline</code> and <code>compute_next_timeout()</code> were both written in the SAME commit (vlang/v#28083 Phase 9), but the aggregator merged <code>idle_timeout_deadline()</code>/<code>loss_detection.next_timeout()</code> and never <code>closing_deadline</code> &mdash; a connection reaching <code>.closing</code>/<code>.draining</code> with nothing else armed returned <code>next_timeout = none</code>, so a real caller-driven event loop would never learn to call <code>process_timeouts()</code> again despite the RFC 9000 &sect;10.2.2 bound being correctly recorded. <strong>Missed TWICE by two separate reviews of the SAME code</strong>: the original Phase 9 <code>/vreview</code> pass never caught it, and a LATER Phase-R fix that added the missing write to <code>closing_deadline</code> (a different, narrower reported gap &mdash; the field was not being SET at all) never traced the field&rsquo;s read side, so the aggregator gap survived untouched underneath a fix that looked complete. Caught only by a maintainer&rsquo;s own separate &ldquo;Local AI Review&rdquo; pass. The integration test also masked it: it always had a stale, never-acked PTO timer armed (the fixture never constructs ACK frames back to the client), so <code>next_timeout</code> was coincidentally non-none even with the bug present &mdash; a same-module white-box test that force-zeroed <code>bytes_in_flight</code> was needed to isolate the true &ldquo;nothing else armed&rdquo; scenario. <strong>Lesson: when a struct has one function whose job is to merge several optional deadline fields into a single &ldquo;next time to act&rdquo; value, ANY new deadline-shaped field added anywhere in that struct is a candidate the aggregator must also merge &mdash; grep the struct for every other <code>?</code>-typed field and confirm each is referenced in the aggregator, every time either side changes.</strong></td>\n      <td><code>detect_scheduler_field_coverage.sh</code></td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">time-unit-scale mismatch (nanoseconds vs milliseconds)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td>Self-caught mid-fix on vlang/v#28083 (net.quic Phase 9): a maintainer &ldquo;Local AI Review&rdquo; finding (idle timer never reset on receive) led to discovering <code>idle_timeout_deadline()</code> combines a <code>time.Duration</code> with <code>now</code> via a bare <code>u64()</code> cast. Working from an assumption carried over from an EARLIER, ALSO-WRONG fix in the same file (<code>closing_or_draining_deadline</code>&rsquo;s pre-existing <code>.milliseconds()</code> call, which looked like established precedent), the ORIGINAL code was &ldquo;corrected&rdquo; to ADD <code>.milliseconds()</code> &mdash; backwards. <code>now</code> is actually a <code>time.sys_mono_now()</code>-sourced nanosecond instant throughout the module (documented in <code>IdleTimeoutState.last_reset</code>&rsquo;s own doc comment, already read but not weighted correctly). The regression test written to Phase-R this even PASSED against the actually-broken pre-fix code on the first attempt, because its own <code>now</code>-delta magnitudes were ALSO chosen under the same wrong millisecond assumption. Settled only by grepping <code>loss_detection_test.v</code>&rsquo;s own pre-existing, already-passing assertions (<code>assert ld.rtt.latest_rtt == 1000 * time.nanosecond</code> for a delta of exactly 1000), independent and unambiguous proof of the true convention. <strong>Lesson: verify a module&rsquo;s time-unit convention via an EXISTING TEST ASSERTION, not by inspecting one sibling function&rsquo;s conversion call &mdash; that sibling can be the first instance of the same mistake, and following it as precedent doubles down instead of catching it.</strong> Fixed both <code>idle_timeout_deadline()</code> (reverted to the original, correct form) and <code>closing_or_draining_deadline</code> (removed the wrong <code>.milliseconds()</code>); sibling-searched the whole module for any other instance, none found.</td>\n      <td>none (semantic fact, not a syntactic pattern -- see G9)</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">new field-line branch (dynamic name reference, insertion unavailable) missing tracking its sibling branches already had</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>qpack_encoder.v</code> <code>encode_field_section</code>'s dynamic-name-reference literal branch (used when a field's name matches an acknowledged dynamic entry but <code>can_insert</code> refuses insertion) never called <code>add_ref</code>, never appended to <code>referenced</code>, never bumped <code>required_insert_count</code> &mdash; unlike the exact-match and post-insert branches in the SAME function, a few lines away, which already did all three. Produced a field section that could declare Required Insert Count 0 while still containing a live dynamic-table reference, rejected by any correct decoder as QPACK_DECOMPRESSION_FAILED (RFC 9204 &sect;2.2.3). GPT-5.6 Luna review on PR #28103 (2026-08-16, pasted directly by the user, no formal PR thread). <strong>Root cause: exactly the class of gap Phase 0 step 7's sibling-parity search targets &mdash; three branches of the same function emit a dynamic-table-referencing representation, and only two of them were checked for the same tracking obligation during this project's own /vreview pass on this exact diff earlier the same session.</strong> Fixed: the third branch now add_refs, tracks, and bumps RIC identically to its siblings. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_phase_r_dynamic_name_reference_without_insertion_tracks_required_insert_count</code>, Phase-R'd against unfixed code before the fix landed (reproduced the exact QPACK_DECOMPRESSION_FAILED the finding predicted).</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- reinforces existing Phase 0 step 7 (sibling search within one function's own branches, not just across files)</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">truncated-vs-malformed conflation in a resumable decoder, letting bad peer data hang instead of erroring</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>qpack_primitives.v</code>'s <code>decode_prefixed_int</code>/<code>decode_prefixed_string</code> had two genuinely distinct failure classes &mdash; \"not enough bytes buffered yet\" (resumable, expected) and \"these bytes are already invalid\" (an oversized integer past this implementation's own limit, malformed Huffman coding) &mdash; but both raised a bare <code>error(...)</code>, indistinguishable to any caller. <code>decode_qpack_encoder_instruction</code>/<code>decode_qpack_decoder_instruction</code> then blanket-converted EVERY failure, truncated or malformed alike, into <code>none</code> (\"wait for more data\") via their own Option-only return type, so a malformed instruction from a broken or malicious peer was indistinguishable from a legitimately incomplete one &mdash; the decoder would retry forever instead of raising QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR and closing the connection (RFC 9204 &sect;2.2.3, &sect;3.2.2). GPT-5.6 Luna review on PR #28103 (2026-08-16). <strong>Root cause: a resumable/incremental parser (this project's own established \"return none, not error, on truncation\" pattern from Phase 10's H3FrameDecoder) is only safe to build this way when the underlying primitive genuinely has no distinct malformed-data failure mode &mdash; true for QUIC's own varint (h3_stream_type.v's existing or-return-none remains correct, re-verified during this same fix), false the moment an implementation adds its OWN additional limit (this project's qpack_max_prefixed_int/qpack_max_string_literal_len caps) on top of the wire format's own theoretical range.</strong> Fixed with a private sentinel error code distinguishing the two classes at the primitive level, and both instruction decoders redesigned from Option-returning to a result-struct + real error propagation, mirroring Phase 10's H3FrameDecoder shape. Fixed identically in BOTH instruction decoders &mdash; the decoder-stream sibling had the exact same bug shape with zero production callers yet, fixed anyway per the sibling-search discipline rather than stopping at the one instance actually reported. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_phase_r_malformed_huffman_value_is_not_silently_treated_as_incomplete</code>, <code>test_decode_qpack_encoder_instruction_propagates_oversized_integer_error</code>, <code>test_phase_r_decoder_instruction_malformed_oversized_integer_propagates_error</code> &mdash; Phase-R'd against unfixed code before the fix landed.</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- the underlying question (does a \"return none on any error\" resumable-parser wrapper actually verify the wrapped primitive has no distinct malformed-data failure mode?) is semantic, not a syntactic grep</td>\n    </tr>",
"<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">capacity-reduction eviction path had no evictable-only guard, unlike its sibling insertion path</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>qpack_encoder.v</code> <code>QpackEncoder.set_capacity</code> called <code>dynamic_table.set_capacity()</code> unconditionally, which evicts oldest-first purely on <code>cur_size &gt; capacity</code> with no reference/acknowledgment check at all &mdash; unlike <code>insert()</code>, which goes through <code>can_insert()</code> first specifically to simulate eviction safety (RFC 9204 &sect;2.1.1) before committing. Reducing capacity could silently evict an entry a not-yet-acknowledged field section still depended on. GPT-5.6 Luna review on PR #28103, round 2 (2026-08-18, pasted directly by the user). <strong>Root cause: this exact function existed unchanged since Phase 11's original commit and was never re-examined against <code>insert</code>'s sibling pattern in either the original implementation or the round-1 Luna fix's own sibling sweep, which was scoped to a different bug's reference-tracking shape, not to every OTHER function touching the dynamic table.</strong> Fixed by adding <code>QpackDynamicTable.can_set_capacity</code>, mirroring <code>can_insert</code>'s simulate-eviction pattern, called from the encoder-level <code>set_capacity</code> before applying a reduction. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_phase_r_set_capacity_rejects_reducing_below_a_referenced_entry</code>, Phase-R'd against unfixed code before the fix landed (reproduced the predicted silent eviction).</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- reinforces existing Phase 0 step 7 (sibling search across functions of the same class, not just across files)</td>\n    </tr>",
"<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">sibling encode/decode functions diverged on an implementation-limit guard; only one side had it</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>qpack_field_line.v</code>'s <code>encode_ric</code> computed <code>full_range := 2 * qpack_max_entries(max_table_capacity)</code> and divided by it with no zero check; <code>decode_ric</code>, two lines below in the same file, already had a <code>full_range == 0</code> guard for the identical computation. <code>qpack_max_entries</code> returns 0 whenever <code>max_table_capacity &lt; 32</code> (Luna's report cited &lt;64, off by the factor-of-2 in <code>full_range</code>'s own definition &mdash; corrected during triage), a realistic input since <code>SETTINGS_QPACK_MAX_TABLE_CAPACITY</code> is peer-controlled. Crashed the whole process on modulo-by-zero instead of returning a graceful error. GPT-5.6 Luna review on PR #28103, round 2 (2026-08-18). <strong>Root cause: the RFC 9204 &sect;4.5.1.1 pseudocode both functions transcribe never states this bound explicitly (it documents the FORMULA, not the degenerate-input guard), so a \"verify against RFC pseudocode\" reading reproduces the RFC's own implicit-range assumption instead of surfacing the gap &mdash; only a direct encode-vs-decode sibling diff, not a spec-conformance reading, would have caught it.</strong> Fixed identically to <code>decode_ric</code>'s guard, rippling <code>encode_ric</code>/<code>encode_field_section_prefix</code>/<code>encode_field_section</code> to Result-returning signatures. <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_phase_r_encode_ric_rejects_capacity_too_small_instead_of_dividing_by_zero</code>, Phase-R'd against unfixed code before the fix landed (reproduced the exact \"modulo by zero\" panic).</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- reinforces Phase 0 step 7's sibling-parity diff, applied to an encode/decode pair rather than two branches of one function</td>\n    </tr>",
"<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">new function's own tail case was wrong relative to the sibling it was built to mirror &mdash; caught by the review's own mandatory contract check</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>qpack_dynamic_table.v</code>'s new <code>can_set_capacity</code> eviction-simulation loop returned bare <code>true</code> whenever it exited normally, instead of <code>can_insert</code>'s own tail (<code>return freed &gt;= needed</code>) &mdash; wrong for any negative <code>new_capacity</code>. Unreachable via the only current caller (<code>QpackEncoder.set_capacity</code>, which only ever passes an already-validated non-negative value), but <code>can_set_capacity</code> is <code>pub</code> and was written as if that caller-side guarantee didn't exist. Self-caught during this session's own <code>/vreview</code> pass on the P1 fix above (2026-08-19), via the mandatory fresh-contract-from-scratch + sibling-parity-diff check required for any NEW function. <strong>Why logged despite not being a miss: it demonstrates the sibling-parity-diff step catching a real defect in freshly-written code within the SAME turn it was written, before any external reviewer saw it &mdash; most rows in this corpus are the opposite case, so this one is worth keeping as a positive data point.</strong> <strong>Repro:</strong> <code>test_dynamic_table_can_set_capacity_rejects_negative_capacity_even_when_table_empty</code>, Phase-R'd against the just-written (not yet committed) buggy code before the fix landed.</td>\n      <td>no new detector -- this IS the existing Phase 0 step 6/7 check working as intended</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">verified the wrong shell-quoting property (word-splitting) instead of the one the reviewer actually flagged (parameter/command substitution)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td><code>heap_attr_struct_alloc_test.v</code>'s regression tests build/run V3 by interpolating a repo-relative path straight into an <code>os.execute()</code> shell command string, wrapped in double quotes for what looked like adequate shell-safety. Double quotes suppress word-splitting (a path containing a space wouldn't break the argument) but do nothing to stop parameter or command substitution &mdash; a repo path containing a literal <code>$</code> (e.g. <code>/tmp/repo$HOME</code>) is expanded by the shell BEFORE the compiler ever sees the <code>-path</code> flag, silently corrupting it to something like <code>/tmp/repo/root</code>. Codex review on PR #28185 (pullrequestreview-5044197146, reviewed commit <code>eaf0b3f973</code>) flagged this directly: the prior round's quoting fix had effectively only been checked against the word-splitting case, not the actual failure surface quoting was supposed to close. Fixed by wrapping the interpolated path in <code>os.quoted_path(...)</code>. <strong>Repro:</strong> a repository path containing <code>$HOME</code>-shaped text, run through the pre-fix command string, silently resolves to the wrong directory instead of erroring or building from the intended path.</td>\n      <td>no dedicated detector yet &mdash; matches the general shell-quoting class the new Angle A bullet now names explicitly</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">third round of the same file patching a THIRD shell-quoting property instead of eliminating the shell dependency itself</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A</span></td>\n      <td>Same file, next round: after the previous fix quoted the interpolated path with <code>os.quoted_path()</code> (closing the word-splitting gap, then the parameter/command-substitution gap from the row above), Codex review on PR #28185 (pullrequestreview-5077561236, reviewed commit <code>29b411dc6f</code>) found a THIRD, Windows-specific shell-quoting property still open: <code>os.execute()</code> on Windows delegates to <code>cmd.exe</code> (<code>vlib/os/os_windows.c.v</code>), and <code>os.quoted_path()</code> only adds a pair of double quotes &mdash; it does not suppress <code>cmd.exe</code>'s own <code>%VAR%</code> percent-expansion, so a repository or temp path containing a literal <code>%TEMP%</code>-shaped segment was still corrupted, across all four shell commands the test file builds. Rather than patch a fourth quoting property whenever a fifth shell-specific expansion rule surfaces, the fix eliminated the shell dependency entirely: switched to the argv-based <code>v3.cmdexec.run()</code> API (already used by <code>secure_command_test.v</code>), which never invokes a shell to interpret the path at all. Fixed in commit <code>84acd375bf</code>. <strong>Escalation rule this round produced:</strong> two consecutive rounds each finding a NEW shell-quoting property on the same file is itself a repeat-offender signal &mdash; stop patching individual properties and switch to an argv-based exec API rather than waiting for a fourth round to find yet another one.</td>\n      <td>no dedicated detector yet &mdash; matches the general shell-quoting class the new Angle A bullet now names explicitly</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">backend-sibling parity of a function the diff USED but never TOUCHED (OpenSSL <code>bytes()</code> minimal-length vs mbedTLS curve-width)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">D</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#28507 (<code>39f56879ea</code>, JalonSolov \"Local AI\"): the new <code>fixed_size: true</code> reload round-trip test failed nondeterministically under <code>-d use_openssl</code> for P-521 because <code>ecdsa_d_use_openssl.v</code>&#39;s <code>PrivateKey.bytes()</code> sized a <code>.fixed</code> key from <code>BN_num_bits()</code> (minimal length, ~1/2 of P-521 keys one byte short) while the mbedTLS backend returned curve width. The review&#39;s sibling table compared only <code>new_key_from_seed</code> across backends, never the <code>bytes()</code> its round trip depends on; and the OpenSSL suite was declared &quot;not run &mdash; no headers&quot; when WSL had OpenSSL all along. Second self-caught finding in the Regression re-review: the fix hand-rolled <code>(EVP_PKEY_get_bits+7)/8</code> beside an existing <code>evp_key_size()</code>.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_ecdsa_fixed_bytes_minimal_len.sh</code> (exit 1 on <code>39f56879ea</code>, 0 after <code>44d01f4af4</code>) + regression test <code>test_new_key_from_seed_fixed_size_leading_zero_seed_keeps_width</code>; rule: never declare OpenSSL &quot;not run&quot; on this box &mdash; use the WSL worktree.</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">hand-rolled validator weaker than the authoritative one (rune-literal UTF-8 merge checked only the continuation-byte range)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">0/7</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#28882 (<code>9c4c196d12</code>, JalonSolov \"Local AI Findings\" P1): <code>char_literal_utf8_escapes_end</code> (<code>vlib/v/scanner/scanner.v</code>) merged byte escapes into one rune when each continuation byte was 0x80..0xBF, so overlong (<code>\\xe0\\x80\\x80</code>, <code>\\xf0\\x80\\x80\\x80</code>), surrogate (<code>\\xed\\xa0\\x80</code>) and &gt;U+10FFFF (<code>\\xf4\\x90\\x80\\x80</code>) sequences compiled. Phase 0 step 7 (mirror the authoritative parser, here <code>encoding.utf8</code>) would have caught it, but <code>/vreview</code> was never run on that commit; the tests were five hand-picked examples and the PR description claimed overlong sequences stayed rejected from the one <code>C0</code> case. Second miss in the same round: tool-call parameters decode JSON escapes, so <code>\\u0061</code> reached a committed test and issue #28880 as a plain <code>a</code>.</td>\n      <td>regression test <code>test_char_literal_byte_escapes_are_one_character_only_when_well_formed_utf8</code> (oracle <code>encoding.utf8.validate_str</code>; fails on <code>9c4c196d12</code>, each partial fix fails on its own class); rule: <code>/vreview</code> before every commit, and grep written bytes for escapes after writing through a tool.</td>\n    </tr>","<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">side effect placed after a chained call that may not return (Boehm abort handler printed after GC_default_on_abort)</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">A/0-7</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#28914 (<code>47d8482248</code>, medvednikov pullrequestreview-5309842842, P2): <code>internal_gc_abort_to_stderr</code> (<code>vlib/builtin/builtin_d_gcboehm.c.v</code>) called Boehm's saved <code>GC_default_on_abort(NULL)</code> before printing; with <code>GC_LOOP_ON_ABORT</code> set that call spins forever (<code>thirdparty/libgc/gc.c:27419-27429</code>), so the fatal message never reached stderr: empty with tcc's prebuilt <code>libgc.a</code>, only Boehm's pointer-suffixed line with gcc. Phase 0 step 7 (cross-layer sibling: Boehm's own handler prints first, loops last) would have shown the order; the code's comment even named <code>GC_LOOP_ON_ABORT</code>. Second miss in the same round: a WSL <code>ls -d X || git worktree add</code> followed by <code>git apply</code> wrote into another session's same-named worktree.</td>\n      <td>regression test <code>test_boehm_abort_is_reported_before_gc_loop_on_abort</code> (fails on <code>47d8482248</code> with tcc and gcc, passes on <code>bbb65c23b2</code> and on Linux); Angle A rule &ldquo;side effect after a chained call that may not return&rdquo; (rev 61).</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">detector read only the committed diff, so uncommitted and untracked unsafe blocks passed clean</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">G</span></td>\n      <td><code>detect_unsafe_blocks.sh</code> listed files with <code>git diff --name-only BASE..HEAD</code> and grepped only for the literal <code>unsafe {</code>. An uncommitted <code>unsafe {</code> in a tracked file and an untracked <code>unsafe{</code> file both exited 0 on a throwaway fixture repo. Self-found while checking uncommitted work; no external PR.</td>\n      <td><code>detect_unsafe_blocks.sh</code>: scan tracked files that differ from BASE (committed or uncommitted) plus untracked files; match <code>unsafe[[:space:]]*\\{</code>. Replay: an uncommitted <code>unsafe{</code> block must exit 1; a bad base must exit 2.</td>\n    </tr>",
  "<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">verification gate diverged from the build it validates: makev.bat smoke test compiled with the default C compiler, not the -cc that built v_up.exe</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#30278 pullrequestreview-5531629970 (medvednikov), reviewed commit <code>531110823f</code>. <code>:smoke_test_updated</code> (<code>makev.bat:684</code>) ran <code>v_up.exe -o x examples/hello_world.v</code> with no <code>-cc</code>; the V3 driver's implicit C compiler on Windows is the bundled TCC, then a system TCC, then gcc, never MSVC. A <code>makev.bat -msvc --local</code> clone has no bundled TCC (<code>--local</code> skips <code>download_tcc</code>), so <code>v_up.exe</code> built with <code>-cc msvc</code>, then the smoke compile failed its C build, fell back to V1 with <code>retrying with</code> in the log, and <code>smoke_log_is_clean</code> refused the install. Existing rule: Phase 0 step 7 &ldquo;every OTHER CONSUMER of a config field&rdquo;; why it failed: its wording covers a struct field whose default changed, not a gate step as a sibling of the build it approves, so the new routine got no parity row.</td>\n      <td>Regression test <code>test_install_smoke_tests_with_the_toolchain_that_built_the_compiler</code> in <code>cmd/tools/makev_test.py</code>: runs all five install routes with stub compilers recording <code>-cc</code>/<code>-cflags</code> and a new-compiler stub that only builds with the toolchain that built it. 5/5 subtests fail on <code>531110823f</code>, pass on 5e803df677. Replay: <code>python cmd/tools/makev_test.py -k smoke_tests_with_the_toolchain</code>. New rule: Phase 0 step 7 paragraph &ldquo;the sibling can be the VERIFICATION step of the artifact it checks&rdquo; (rev 63).</td>\n    </tr>",
"<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">renamed emitted symbol, references not renamed: fastc entry became wmain at its definition while calls and function values of main still resolved to main</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">C</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#30672 pullrequestreview-5541265161 (medvednikov), reviewed commit <code>d2c3084ada</code>. <code>parse_function</code> (<code>vlib/v/gen/fastc/fn.v:920</code>) special-cased <code>is_main</code> to emit <code>int wmain(void)</code> on Windows, but <code>fn again() { main() }</code> and <code>f := main</code> rendered <code>main</code> through <code>c_function_name_for_key('main')</code>, an undefined symbol under the MinGW <code>-municode</code> startup the PR targeted. Existing rule: Angle C &ldquo;grep for its callers&rdquo;; why it failed: it was run on the V callers of the changed functions, while the renamed thing was the emitted C name, whose callers are the generator&rsquo;s own reference renderers.</td>\n      <td>Regression test <code>vlib/v/gen/fastc/windows_entry_call_test.v</code>: a <code>main()</code> call and a <code>main</code> function value must render as <code>wmain</code> for a Windows target and <code>main</code> for Linux; a test build keeps the ordinary <code>v_fastc_program_main</code>. Fails on <code>d2c3084ada</code>, passes on a7657b1478, where <code>c_function_name_or</code> owns the entry name. New rule: Angle C bullet &ldquo;a renamed EMITTED symbol has callers too&rdquo; (rev 64).</td>\n    </tr>",
"<tr>\n      <td><span class=\"class-label\">one flag source ignored and gate placed ahead of non-building modes: the MSVC-ABI clang rejection read -cflags but not CFLAGS, and refused -print-v-files/-print-watched-files</span></td>\n      <td><span class=\"angle-ref\">Phase 0 step 7 / D</span></td>\n      <td>vlang/v#30687 pullrequestreview-5556067468 (medvednikov), reviewed commit <code>09eaae63cd</code>. The retarget exemption checked <code>user_c_flags</code> only, while every compile-arg builder in <code>driver.v</code> starts from <code>environment_c_flags</code>; and the gate sat right after compiler selection, before the source-listing modes return. Existing rules: Phase 0 step 7 &ldquo;every other consumer of a config field&rdquo; and Angle D guard completeness; why they failed: the sibling table compared the gate with the -prod picker only, and the exemption list was asserted, not derived from the early returns.</td>\n      <td>Regression test <code>vlib/v/driver/windows_clang_msvc_abi_test.v</code> (stub clang reporting <code>x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code>): listings succeed, a plain build is rejected, a <code>CFLAGS</code>-retargeted build reaches the compiler. Fails on <code>09eaae63cd</code>. Fix shape: the gate moved beside <code>msvc_require_cl</code>, the sibling that runs only once a C build is required.</td>\n    </tr>"
];
